t. But in compile mode, the system is deferred. When it binds a token to its meaning, it intentionally delays the collapse. Oh, I see. Instead of executing the payload, it appends an execution token, a pointer, into the memory of the word it is actively constructing. It's just layering more binds into the history, building up the potential energy of H3, saving the execution collapse for whenever the new word is eventually called. So the entire architectural difference between an interpreter and a compiler boils down to whether the machine decides to collapse the semantic bind immediately or defer it to the output stream. It's the exact same machinery, just operating with a different topological delay. Yes. And this brings us to the final deep technical mechanism in the paper, threading models. Let's get into threading. When we talk about appending execution tokens to a compiled word, we are talking about threading. How does the instruction pointer actually traverse from one compiled word to the next? Fliction analyzes indirect threading, direct threading, and subroutine threading. Let's break these down because they have massive implications for CPU performance and cache locality. Let's start with indirect threading, which is the classic historical fourth model. In indirect threading, you have an extra layer of indirection. A compiled word is essentially an array of memory addresses, but those addresses don't point directly to machine code. They point to an execution token, which is a structural header, and that header contains a pointer to the actual executable machine code. So the CPU has to dereference a pointer, read the address, jump to the header, read another pointer, and then finally jump to the code. That double jump is computationally expensive. It guarantees cache misses on modern processors. It does. Which is why direct threading was developed. Direct threading removes the middleman. The compiled array of addresses points straight to the executable machine code fields. It eliminates one dereference cycle, significantly improving performance. And subroutine threading takes it to the ultimate hardware extreme. Yes. In subroutine threading, the compiler abandons the internal array of pointers completely. Instead, it emits raw native machine code. It emits native by ASICs or ARM call instructions. It delegates the control flow entirely to the hardware processor's internal return stack. This is where you get maximum speed, because you are leveraging the silicon's native branch predictors and instruction caches. But why does Flixion dedicate so much space to these threading models in a paper about theoretical primitives? Yeah. What do by-86 call instructions have to do with SpherePop? Because Flixion mathematically proves that you do not need different theories to explain these wildly different hardware behaviors. In the SpherePop calculus, each threading model simply represents a different quantifiable number of reachability transitions between the current instruction pointer and the final semantic transformation. So indirect threading is just a topological space with one additional POP-like dereference layer built into its structure. Direct threading mathematically contracts that layer, collapsing the topology. And subroutine threading delegates the collapse rule directly to the hardware's Michael code. Exactly. It unifies the gritty, physical realities of hardware implementation with the abstract mathematical purity of the SpherePop model. It proves that the geometry holds up all the way down to the bare metal. I have to say, walking through the complete architecture of this paper is staggering. We started with four seemingly simple actions. Commit to a state, mark something as inadmissible without deleting it, couple two states together, and view the history through a flattened projection. Pop, refuse, bind, and collapse. Right. And simply by layering those, we mathematically derive memory sharing, instant state roll