sion_ratio": 1.5059760956175299, "no_speech_prob": 1.882962535892929e-12}, {"id": 29, "seek": 10456, "start": 111.48, "end": 115.32000000000001, "text": " Millions of lines of code, intermediate representations, optimization passes.", "tokens": [50711, 7190, 626, 295, 3876, 295, 3089, 11, 19376, 33358, 11, 19618, 11335, 13, 50903], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11628500263342696, "compression_ratio": 1.5059760956175299, "no_speech_prob": 1.882962535892929e-12}, {"id": 30, "seek": 10456, "start": 115.52000000000001, "end": 117.04, "text": " Yeah, they are insanely complex.", "tokens": [50913, 865, 11, 436, 366, 40965, 3997, 13, 50989], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11628500263342696, "compression_ratio": 1.5059760956175299, "no_speech_prob": 1.882962535892929e-12}, {"id": 31, "seek": 10456, "start": 117.16, "end": 119.72, "text": " But Fliction is proposing something radically different here.", "tokens": [50995, 583, 3235, 4105, 307, 29939, 746, 35508, 819, 510, 13, 51123], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11628500263342696, "compression_ratio": 1.5059760956175299, "no_speech_prob": 1.882962535892929e-12}, {"id": 32, "seek": 10456, "start": 119.88, "end": 123.9, "text": " Basically finding the quantum level of computer science and building upward from there.", "tokens": [51131, 8537, 5006, 264, 13018, 1496, 295, 3820, 3497, 293, 2390, 23452, 490, 456, 13, 51332], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11628500263342696, "compression_ratio": 1.5059760956175299, "no_speech_prob": 1.882962535892929e-12}, {"id": 33, "seek": 12390, "start": 123.9, "end": 129.96, "text": " Yeah, and to really appreciate what Fliction is doing, we have to kind of recalibrate our baseline assumptions about syntax.", "tokens": [50365, 865, 11, 293, 281, 534, 4449, 437, 3235, 4105, 307, 884, 11, 321, 362, 281, 733, 295, 850, 304, 897, 4404, 527, 20518, 17695, 466, 28431, 13, 50668], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12325618450458233, "compression_ratio": 1.3519553072625698, "no_speech_prob": 1.4950843297764638e-12}, {"id": 34, "seek": 12390, "start": 130.34, "end": 137.9, "text": " Like, if you look at standard languages, Python, Rust, C, they rely heavily on abstract syntax trees, or ASTs.", "tokens": [50687, 1743, 11, 498, 291, 574, 412, 3832, 8650, 11, 15329, 11, 34952, 11, 383, 11, 436, 10687, 10950, 322, 12649, 28431, 5852, 11, 420, 316, 6840, 82, 13, 51065], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12325618450458233, "compression_ratio": 1.3519553072625698, "no_speech_prob": 1.4950843297764638e-12}, {"id": 35, "seek": 12390, "start": 138.74, "end": 138.96, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51107, 1779, 13, 51118], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12325618450458233, "compression_ratio": 1.3519553072625698, "no_speech_prob": 1.4950843297764638e-12}, {"id": 36, "seek": 13896, "start": 138.96, "end": 144.38, "text": " The code you write is a heavily nested, structured tree, even if it looks flat on the screen to you.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 3089, 291, 2464, 307, 257, 10950, 15646, 292, 11, 18519, 4230, 11, 754, 498, 309, 1542, 4962, 322, 264, 2568, 281, 291, 13, 50636], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990539756981102, "compression_ratio": 1.6571428571428573, "no_speech_prob": 1.927924833666772e-12}, {"id": 37, "seek": 13896, "start": 145.10000000000002, "end": 151.14000000000001, "text": " The compiler's first major job is to tear apart your strings of text, figure out the precedents of operations,", "tokens": [50672, 440, 31958, 311, 700, 2563, 1691, 307, 281, 12556, 4936, 428, 13985, 295, 2487, 11, 2573, 484, 264, 16969, 791, 295, 7705, 11, 50974], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990539756981102, "compression_ratio": 1.6571428571428573, "no_speech_prob": 1.927924833666772e-12}, {"id": 38, "seek": 13896, "start": 151.48000000000002, "end": 157.14000000000001, "text": " and build this massive in-memory tree just to understand the order of execution.", "tokens": [50991, 293, 1322, 341, 5994, 294, 12, 17886, 827, 4230, 445, 281, 1223, 264, 1668, 295, 15058, 13, 51274], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990539756981102, "compression_ratio": 1.6571428571428573, "no_speech_prob": 1.927924833666772e-12}, {"id": 39, "seek": 13896, "start": 157.60000000000002, "end": 162.38, "text": " Right, because in a standard language, the text itself hides the order of operations.", "tokens": [51297, 1779, 11, 570, 294, 257, 3832, 2856, 11, 264, 2487, 2564, 35953, 264, 1668, 295, 7705, 13, 51536], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990539756981102, "compression_ratio": 1.6571428571428573, "no_speech_prob": 1.927924833666772e-12}, {"id": 40, "seek": 13896, "start": 162.84, "end": 166.22, "text": " Like, if you're building a complex expression, the compiler has to traverse the tree,", "tokens": [51559, 1743, 11, 498, 291, 434, 2390, 257, 3997, 6114, 11, 264, 31958, 575, 281, 45674, 264, 4230, 11, 51728], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990539756981102, "compression_ratio": 1.6571428571428573, "no_speech_prob": 1.927924833666772e-12}, {"id": 41, "seek": 16622, "start": 166.22, "end": 170.48, "text": " figure out that an inner function call has to resolve before an outer multiplication can happen,", "tokens": [50365, 2573, 484, 300, 364, 7284, 2445, 818, 575, 281, 14151, 949, 364, 10847, 27290, 393, 1051, 11, 50578], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 42, "seek": 16622, "start": 170.88, "end": 172.52, "text": " and, you know, allocate registers accordingly.", "tokens": [50598, 293, 11, 291, 458, 11, 35713, 38351, 19717, 13, 50680], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 43, "seek": 16622, "start": 172.66, "end": 173.08, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50687, 7587, 13, 50708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 44, "seek": 16622, "start": 173.2, "end": 178.4, "text": " It's like having a completed cake recipe where you have to guess the order of the steps just by looking at the finished cake.", "tokens": [50714, 467, 311, 411, 1419, 257, 7365, 5908, 6782, 689, 291, 362, 281, 2041, 264, 1668, 295, 264, 4439, 445, 538, 1237, 412, 264, 4335, 5908, 13, 50974], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 45, "seek": 16622, "start": 179.07999999999998, "end": 182.78, "text": " It's a huge amount of overhead before any actual computation takes place.", "tokens": [51008, 467, 311, 257, 2603, 2372, 295, 19922, 949, 604, 3539, 24903, 2516, 1081, 13, 51193], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 46, "seek": 16622, "start": 183.12, "end": 188.36, "text": " That's a really good analogy, and that is exactly why Fliction pairs the SpherePop machine with Forth,", "tokens": [51210, 663, 311, 257, 534, 665, 21663, 11, 293, 300, 307, 2293, 983, 3235, 4105, 15494, 264, 318, 6605, 38677, 3479, 365, 1171, 392, 11, 51472], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 47, "seek": 16622, "start": 188.66, "end": 191.82, "text": " because Forth completely bypasses the AST.", "tokens": [51487, 570, 1171, 392, 2584, 24996, 279, 264, 316, 6840, 13, 51645], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 48, "seek": 16622, "start": 192.07999999999998, "end": 195.62, "text": " It operates on reverse Polish notation, or RPN.", "tokens": [51658, 467, 22577, 322, 9943, 18504, 24657, 11, 420, 14105, 45, 13, 51835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14839977526482734, "compression_ratio": 1.6595744680851063, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610557551476445e-12}, {"id": 49, "seek": 19562, "start": 195.62, "end": 196.46, "text": " Right, RPN.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 11, 14105, 45, 13, 50407], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 50, "seek": 19562, "start": 196.54, "end": 200.48000000000002, "text": " Where you push operands to a stack and then call operators that consume them.", "tokens": [50411, 2305, 291, 2944, 2208, 2967, 281, 257, 8630, 293, 550, 818, 19077, 300, 14732, 552, 13, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 51, "seek": 19562, "start": 200.94, "end": 204.22, "text": " But Fliction isn't just praising RPN for being minimal.", "tokens": [50631, 583, 3235, 4105, 1943, 380, 445, 42941, 14105, 45, 337, 885, 13206, 13, 50795], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 52, "seek": 19562, "start": 204.76, "end": 208.18, "text": " The paper frames RPN as something much more profound.", "tokens": [50822, 440, 3035, 12083, 14105, 45, 382, 746, 709, 544, 14382, 13, 50993], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 53, "seek": 19562, "start": 208.42000000000002, "end": 211.1, "text": " It actually calls it a serialized proof.", "tokens": [51005, 467, 767, 5498, 309, 257, 17436, 1602, 8177, 13, 51139], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 54, "seek": 19562, "start": 211.36, "end": 212.44, "text": " A serialized proof.", "tokens": [51152, 316, 17436, 1602, 8177, 13, 51206], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 55, "seek": 19562, "start": 212.56, "end": 214.32, "text": " That phrase right there is so crucial.", "tokens": [51212, 663, 9535, 558, 456, 307, 370, 11462, 13, 51300], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18417072296142578, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 2.5442959371291263e-12}, {"id": 56, "seek": 21432, "start": 214.32, "end": 219.18, "text": " It is the crux of the entire argument, honestly, because in an AST-based language,", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 264, 5140, 87, 295, 264, 2302, 6770, 11, 6095, 11, 570, 294, 364, 316, 6840, 12, 6032, 2856, 11, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08755937277102004, "compression_ratio": 1.606060606060606, "no_speech_prob": 1.591850674713402e-12}, {"id": 57, "seek": 21432, "start": 219.62, "end": 222.48, "text": " the compiler has to discover the proof of execution order.", "tokens": [50630, 264, 31958, 575, 281, 4411, 264, 8177, 295, 15058, 1668, 13, 50773], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08755937277102004, "compression_ratio": 1.606060606060606, "no_speech_prob": 1.591850674713402e-12}, {"id": 58, "seek": 21432, "start": 223.18, "end": 226.64, "text": " In Forth, the sequence of tokens you provide is already the proof.", "tokens": [50808, 682, 1171, 392, 11, 264, 8310, 295, 22667, 291, 2893, 307, 1217, 264, 8177, 13, 50981], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08755937277102004, "compression_ratio": 1.606060606060606, "no_speech_prob": 1.591850674713402e-12}, {"id": 59, "seek": 21432, "start": 226.73999999999998, "end": 227.14, "text": " Oh, wow.", "tokens": [50986, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 51006], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08755937277102004, "compression_ratio": 1.606060606060606, "no_speech_prob": 1.591850674713402e-12}, {"id": 60, "seek": 21432, "start": 227.26, "end": 227.45999999999998, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51012, 865, 13, 51022], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08755937277102004, "compression_ratio": 1.606060606060606, "no_speech_prob": 1.591850674713402e-12}, {"id": 61, "seek": 21432, "start": 227.64, "end": 237.42, "text": " When you write a sequence in Forth, you are explicitly documenting that the required operands will become available in the exact physical location needed right before the operation that consumes them.", "tokens": [51031, 1133, 291, 2464, 257, 8310, 294, 1171, 392, 11, 291, 366, 20803, 42360, 300, 264, 4739, 2208, 2967, 486, 1813, 2435, 294, 264, 1900, 4001, 4914, 2978, 558, 949, 264, 6916, 300, 48823, 552, 13, 51520], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08755937277102004, "compression_ratio": 1.606060606060606, "no_speech_prob": 1.591850674713402e-12}, {"id": 62, "seek": 23742, "start": 237.42, "end": 237.6, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 50374], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 63, "seek": 23742, "start": 237.66, "end": 238.89999999999998, "text": " It's like a literal conveyor belt.", "tokens": [50377, 467, 311, 411, 257, 20411, 18053, 2454, 10750, 13, 50439], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 64, "seek": 23742, "start": 239.0, "end": 240.98, "text": " Put down flour, put down sugar, mix, bake.", "tokens": [50444, 4935, 760, 7693, 11, 829, 760, 5076, 11, 2890, 11, 16562, 13, 50543], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 65, "seek": 23742, "start": 241.14, "end": 244.0, "text": " You're not relying on the compiler to magically resolve your dependencies.", "tokens": [50551, 509, 434, 406, 24140, 322, 264, 31958, 281, 39763, 14151, 428, 36606, 13, 50694], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 66, "seek": 23742, "start": 244.35999999999999, "end": 248.67999999999998, "text": " You are providing a flat, step-by-step, state machine transition.", "tokens": [50712, 509, 366, 6530, 257, 4962, 11, 1823, 12, 2322, 12, 16792, 11, 1785, 3479, 6034, 13, 50928], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 67, "seek": 23742, "start": 248.89999999999998, "end": 249.38, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [50939, 48746, 736, 13, 50963], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 68, "seek": 23742, "start": 249.76, "end": 253.38, "text": " And this makes Forth uniquely suited to map onto the SpherePop machine.", "tokens": [50982, 400, 341, 1669, 1171, 392, 31474, 24736, 281, 4471, 3911, 264, 318, 6605, 38677, 3479, 13, 51163], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.158891206500174, "compression_ratio": 1.4528301886792452, "no_speech_prob": 1.356484369274824e-12}, {"id": 69, "seek": 25338, "start": 253.38, "end": 262.6, "text": " Fliction argues that in this context, a fourth word, which is what we'd call a function or command in other languages, a word doesn't denote a value.", "tokens": [50365, 3235, 4105, 38218, 300, 294, 341, 4319, 11, 257, 6409, 1349, 11, 597, 307, 437, 321, 1116, 818, 257, 2445, 420, 5622, 294, 661, 8650, 11, 257, 1349, 1177, 380, 45708, 257, 2158, 13, 50826], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 70, "seek": 25338, "start": 263.28, "end": 266.76, "text": " It denotes an admissible reconfiguration of reachable positions.", "tokens": [50860, 467, 1441, 17251, 364, 5910, 41073, 9993, 20646, 8167, 295, 2524, 712, 8432, 13, 51034], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 71, "seek": 25338, "start": 266.76, "end": 268.42, "text": " Okay, let's pull that apart for a second.", "tokens": [51034, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 2235, 300, 4936, 337, 257, 1150, 13, 51117], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 72, "seek": 25338, "start": 268.9, "end": 274.94, "text": " Because admissible reconfiguration of reachable positions is a very dense way of describing a function.", "tokens": [51141, 1436, 5910, 41073, 9993, 20646, 8167, 295, 2524, 712, 8432, 307, 257, 588, 18011, 636, 295, 16141, 257, 2445, 13, 51443], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 73, "seek": 25338, "start": 275.32, "end": 275.92, "text": " It is, yeah.", "tokens": [51462, 467, 307, 11, 1338, 13, 51492], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 74, "seek": 25338, "start": 275.96, "end": 279.44, "text": " If I'm writing a standard application, I think of a function as a black box.", "tokens": [51494, 759, 286, 478, 3579, 257, 3832, 3861, 11, 286, 519, 295, 257, 2445, 382, 257, 2211, 2424, 13, 51668], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 75, "seek": 25338, "start": 280.0, "end": 281.36, "text": " Takes an input, returns an output.", "tokens": [51696, 44347, 364, 4846, 11, 11247, 364, 5598, 13, 51764], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14632240174308656, "compression_ratio": 1.7765567765567765, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552104959751234e-12}, {"id": 76, "seek": 28136, "start": 281.36, "end": 285.72, "text": " But here, we're talking about the topology of the machine state, right?", "tokens": [50365, 583, 510, 11, 321, 434, 1417, 466, 264, 1192, 1793, 295, 264, 3479, 1785, 11, 558, 30, 50583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14062507178193778, "compression_ratio": 1.5863453815261044, "no_speech_prob": 1.8170394659386413e-12}, {"id": 77, "seek": 28136, "start": 285.74, "end": 285.94, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50584, 1779, 13, 50594], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14062507178193778, "compression_ratio": 1.5863453815261044, "no_speech_prob": 1.8170394659386413e-12}, {"id": 78, "seek": 28136, "start": 286.12, "end": 290.0, "text": " You can visualize the machine state as a landscape of accessible memory.", "tokens": [50603, 509, 393, 23273, 264, 3479, 1785, 382, 257, 9661, 295, 9515, 4675, 13, 50797], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14062507178193778, "compression_ratio": 1.5863453815261044, "no_speech_prob": 1.8170394659386413e-12}, {"id": 79, "seek": 28136, "start": 290.32, "end": 299.0, "text": " When an operation executes in Forth, it is fundamentally shifting what data is immediately reachable by the instruction pointer at that specific microsecond.", "tokens": [50813, 1133, 364, 6916, 4454, 1819, 294, 1171, 392, 11, 309, 307, 17879, 17573, 437, 1412, 307, 4258, 2524, 712, 538, 264, 10951, 23918, 412, 300, 2685, 3123, 37841, 18882, 13, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14062507178193778, "compression_ratio": 1.5863453815261044, "no_speech_prob": 1.8170394659386413e-12}, {"id": 80, "seek": 28136, "start": 299.86, "end": 303.90000000000003, "text": " So an operation is like a local contract that maps one landscape to another.", "tokens": [51290, 407, 364, 6916, 307, 411, 257, 2654, 4364, 300, 11317, 472, 9661, 281, 1071, 13, 51492], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14062507178193778, "compression_ratio": 1.5863453815261044, "no_speech_prob": 1.8170394659386413e-12}, {"id": 81, "seek": 28136, "start": 304.1, "end": 304.58000000000004, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51502, 7587, 13, 51526], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14062507178193778, "compression_ratio": 1.5863453815261044, "no_speech_prob": 1.8170394659386413e-12}, {"id": 82, "seek": 30458, "start": 304.58, "end": 312.44, "text": " It promises that if you hand it a specific arrangement of reachable data, it will consume it and produce a new, perfectly defined arrangement.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 16403, 300, 498, 291, 1011, 309, 257, 2685, 17620, 295, 2524, 712, 1412, 11, 309, 486, 14732, 309, 293, 5258, 257, 777, 11, 6239, 7642, 17620, 13, 50758], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13411144206398412, "compression_ratio": 1.4841628959276019, "no_speech_prob": 1.449607794912211e-12}, {"id": 83, "seek": 30458, "start": 312.94, "end": 316.9, "text": " The sequence of words in a Forth program is just a chain of these local contracts.", "tokens": [50783, 440, 8310, 295, 2283, 294, 257, 1171, 392, 1461, 307, 445, 257, 5021, 295, 613, 2654, 13952, 13, 50981], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13411144206398412, "compression_ratio": 1.4841628959276019, "no_speech_prob": 1.449607794912211e-12}, {"id": 84, "seek": 30458, "start": 317.14, "end": 322.9, "text": " Proving that at every single SEP, the required landscape exists before the transformation occurs.", "tokens": [50993, 1705, 798, 300, 412, 633, 2167, 318, 8929, 11, 264, 4739, 9661, 8198, 949, 264, 9887, 11843, 13, 51281], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13411144206398412, "compression_ratio": 1.4841628959276019, "no_speech_prob": 1.449607794912211e-12}, {"id": 85, "seek": 30458, "start": 323.02, "end": 323.4, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51287, 1079, 13, 51306], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13411144206398412, "compression_ratio": 1.4841628959276019, "no_speech_prob": 1.449607794912211e-12}, {"id": 86, "seek": 32340, "start": 323.4, "end": 328.12, "text": " You know, pointing this out for you listening, it drastically reduces the cognitive load.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 458, 11, 12166, 341, 484, 337, 291, 4764, 11, 309, 29673, 18081, 264, 15605, 3677, 13, 50601], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 87, "seek": 32340, "start": 328.23999999999995, "end": 328.38, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50607, 865, 13, 50614], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 88, "seek": 32340, "start": 328.47999999999996, "end": 331.71999999999997, "text": " You don't have to hold a branching, multilayer tree in memory.", "tokens": [50619, 509, 500, 380, 362, 281, 1797, 257, 9819, 278, 11, 2120, 388, 11167, 4230, 294, 4675, 13, 50781], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 89, "seek": 32340, "start": 331.96, "end": 336.03999999999996, "text": " You just track a flat sequence of changes to the reachable landscape.", "tokens": [50793, 509, 445, 2837, 257, 4962, 8310, 295, 2962, 281, 264, 2524, 712, 9661, 13, 50997], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 90, "seek": 32340, "start": 336.32, "end": 337.35999999999996, "text": " It's so much cleaner.", "tokens": [51011, 467, 311, 370, 709, 16532, 13, 51063], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 91, "seek": 32340, "start": 337.84, "end": 341.29999999999995, "text": " But this brings us to the engine actually powering these transformations.", "tokens": [51087, 583, 341, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 2848, 767, 1347, 278, 613, 34852, 13, 51260], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 92, "seek": 32340, "start": 341.9, "end": 346.67999999999995, "text": " If Forth is just moving things on a conveyor belt, what exactly is powering the belt?", "tokens": [51290, 759, 1171, 392, 307, 445, 2684, 721, 322, 257, 18053, 2454, 10750, 11, 437, 2293, 307, 1347, 278, 264, 10750, 30, 51529], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 93, "seek": 32340, "start": 346.79999999999995, "end": 347.02, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51535, 865, 13, 51546], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13750629425048827, "compression_ratio": 1.5817490494296578, "no_speech_prob": 1.537096296598539e-12}, {"id": 94, "seek": 34702, "start": 347.02, "end": 352.15999999999997, "text": " The paper says all of these stack operations are built out of just four irreducible primitives.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 3035, 1619, 439, 295, 613, 8630, 7705, 366, 3094, 484, 295, 445, 1451, 16014, 769, 32128, 2886, 38970, 13, 50622], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 95, "seek": 34702, "start": 352.44, "end": 352.52, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50636, 1779, 13, 50640], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 96, "seek": 34702, "start": 352.64, "end": 357.38, "text": " The four quantum building blocks, pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.", "tokens": [50646, 440, 1451, 13018, 2390, 8474, 11, 1665, 11, 16791, 11, 14786, 11, 293, 15584, 13, 50883], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 97, "seek": 34702, "start": 357.56, "end": 357.74, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50892, 1033, 13, 50901], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 98, "seek": 34702, "start": 357.82, "end": 360.97999999999996, "text": " I have to jump in here and make a crucial clarification based on the text.", "tokens": [50905, 286, 362, 281, 3012, 294, 510, 293, 652, 257, 11462, 34449, 2361, 322, 264, 2487, 13, 51063], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 99, "seek": 34702, "start": 361.09999999999997, "end": 361.21999999999997, "text": " Yep.", "tokens": [51069, 7010, 13, 51075], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 100, "seek": 34702, "start": 361.41999999999996, "end": 363.7, "text": " There's a huge terminological collision here.", "tokens": [51085, 821, 311, 257, 2603, 10761, 4383, 24644, 510, 13, 51199], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 101, "seek": 34702, "start": 364.52, "end": 370.62, "text": " Sphere pops pop with a capital P is not the same as a traditional stack pop with a lowercase p.", "tokens": [51240, 318, 6605, 16795, 1665, 365, 257, 4238, 430, 307, 406, 264, 912, 382, 257, 5164, 8630, 1665, 365, 257, 3126, 9765, 280, 13, 51545], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 102, "seek": 34702, "start": 370.78, "end": 371.38, "text": " Not at all.", "tokens": [51553, 1726, 412, 439, 13, 51583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 103, "seek": 34702, "start": 371.46, "end": 373.58, "text": " It's actually the exact opposite in some ways.", "tokens": [51587, 467, 311, 767, 264, 1900, 6182, 294, 512, 2098, 13, 51693], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 104, "seek": 34702, "start": 373.62, "end": 373.88, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51695, 1779, 13, 51708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14790775979212087, "compression_ratio": 1.596551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 1.471906797834155e-12}, {"id": 105, "seek": 37388, "start": 373.88, "end": 379.74, "text": " In sphere pop, a capital P pop is an axiomatic commitment to a specific option for closing", "tokens": [50365, 682, 16687, 1665, 11, 257, 4238, 430, 1665, 307, 364, 6360, 72, 13143, 8371, 281, 257, 2685, 3614, 337, 10377, 50658], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 106, "seek": 37388, "start": 379.74, "end": 381.38, "text": " other branches of possibility.", "tokens": [50658, 661, 14770, 295, 7959, 13, 50740], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 107, "seek": 37388, "start": 382.02, "end": 387.0, "text": " So when I push a value onto the fourth data stack, I am actually executing a sphere pop.", "tokens": [50772, 407, 562, 286, 2944, 257, 2158, 3911, 264, 6409, 1412, 8630, 11, 286, 669, 767, 32368, 257, 16687, 1665, 13, 51021], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 108, "seek": 37388, "start": 387.38, "end": 387.7, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51040, 1079, 13, 51056], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 109, "seek": 37388, "start": 387.8, "end": 392.78, "text": " You are committing the machine's timeline to a reality where that value now exists in that", "tokens": [51061, 509, 366, 26659, 264, 3479, 311, 12933, 281, 257, 4103, 689, 300, 2158, 586, 8198, 294, 300, 51310], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 110, "seek": 37388, "start": 392.78, "end": 397.15999999999997, "text": " position, closing off the realities where you push something else or nothing at all.", "tokens": [51310, 2535, 11, 10377, 766, 264, 27785, 689, 291, 2944, 746, 1646, 420, 1825, 412, 439, 13, 51529], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 111, "seek": 37388, "start": 397.21999999999997, "end": 400.68, "text": " It is an assertion of existence in the append-only log of the machine.", "tokens": [51532, 467, 307, 364, 19810, 313, 295, 9123, 294, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 3565, 295, 264, 3479, 13, 51705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 112, "seek": 37388, "start": 401.26, "end": 401.54, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [51734, 1033, 13, 51748], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 113, "seek": 37388, "start": 401.71999999999997, "end": 402.34, "text": " That makes sense.", "tokens": [51757, 663, 1669, 2020, 13, 51788], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1065437890649811, "compression_ratio": 1.7112676056338028, "no_speech_prob": 1.4953894242678012e-12}, {"id": 114, "seek": 40234, "start": 402.34, "end": 403.82, "text": " So what about the second primitive?", "tokens": [50365, 407, 437, 466, 264, 1150, 28540, 30, 50439], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12231579693880948, "compression_ratio": 1.5166666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.573301710265651e-12}, {"id": 115, "seek": 40234, "start": 403.82, "end": 409.5, "text": " The second primitive, refuse, is where the append-only nature really shows its teeth.", "tokens": [50439, 440, 1150, 28540, 11, 16791, 11, 307, 689, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 3687, 534, 3110, 1080, 7798, 13, 50723], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12231579693880948, "compression_ratio": 1.5166666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.573301710265651e-12}, {"id": 116, "seek": 40234, "start": 410.44, "end": 416.09999999999997, "text": " Refuse means marking an option or a value as inadmissible for future operations.", "tokens": [50770, 16957, 438, 1355, 25482, 364, 3614, 420, 257, 2158, 382, 42148, 76, 41073, 337, 2027, 7705, 13, 51053], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12231579693880948, "compression_ratio": 1.5166666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.573301710265651e-12}, {"id": 117, "seek": 40234, "start": 416.34, "end": 420.78, "text": " But it explicitly forbids erasing the historical record of that value.", "tokens": [51065, 583, 309, 20803, 16603, 3742, 1189, 3349, 264, 8584, 2136, 295, 300, 2158, 13, 51287], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12231579693880948, "compression_ratio": 1.5166666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.573301710265651e-12}, {"id": 118, "seek": 42078, "start": 420.78, "end": 421.21999999999997, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 13, 50387], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439987246909838, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.6875386721695862e-12}, {"id": 119, "seek": 42078, "start": 421.41999999999996, "end": 424.03999999999996, "text": " This is where I have to look at the practical reality of hardware.", "tokens": [50397, 639, 307, 689, 286, 362, 281, 574, 412, 264, 8496, 4103, 295, 8837, 13, 50528], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439987246909838, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.6875386721695862e-12}, {"id": 120, "seek": 42078, "start": 424.26, "end": 429.9, "text": " If I am running, say, a high-frequency trading application or a tight cryptographic loop,", "tokens": [50539, 759, 286, 669, 2614, 11, 584, 11, 257, 1090, 12, 19325, 48154, 9529, 3861, 420, 257, 4524, 9844, 12295, 6367, 11, 50821], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439987246909838, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.6875386721695862e-12}, {"id": 121, "seek": 42078, "start": 429.97999999999996, "end": 434.61999999999995, "text": " and I am continuously pushing values and then popping them in the traditional sense, consuming", "tokens": [50825, 293, 286, 669, 15684, 7380, 4190, 293, 550, 18374, 552, 294, 264, 5164, 2020, 11, 19867, 51057], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439987246909838, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.6875386721695862e-12}, {"id": 122, "seek": 42078, "start": 434.61999999999995, "end": 437.84, "text": " them, the sphere pop model says I never actually deleted them.", "tokens": [51057, 552, 11, 264, 16687, 1665, 2316, 1619, 286, 1128, 767, 22981, 552, 13, 51218], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439987246909838, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.6875386721695862e-12}, {"id": 123, "seek": 42078, "start": 437.94, "end": 440.4, "text": " I just layered a refuse command over them.", "tokens": [51223, 286, 445, 34666, 257, 16791, 5622, 670, 552, 13, 51346], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439987246909838, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.6875386721695862e-12}, {"id": 124, "seek": 44040, "start": 440.4, "end": 445.96, "text": " If I do that millions of times a second, I am blowing out my L1 and L2 cache immediately.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 286, 360, 300, 6803, 295, 1413, 257, 1150, 11, 286, 669, 15068, 484, 452, 441, 16, 293, 441, 17, 19459, 4258, 13, 50643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1867101643536542, "compression_ratio": 1.4519230769230769, "no_speech_prob": 1.83246451866681e-12}, {"id": 125, "seek": 44040, "start": 446.38, "end": 451.32, "text": " I am going to consume gigabytes of RAM just storing the history of discarded integers.", "tokens": [50664, 286, 669, 516, 281, 14732, 42741, 295, 14561, 445, 26085, 264, 2503, 295, 45469, 41674, 13, 50911], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1867101643536542, "compression_ratio": 1.4519230769230769, "no_speech_prob": 1.83246451866681e-12}, {"id": 126, "seek": 44040, "start": 451.59999999999997, "end": 453.41999999999996, "text": " Doesn't the machine run out of space?", "tokens": [50925, 12955, 380, 264, 3479, 1190, 484, 295, 1901, 30, 51016], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1867101643536542, "compression_ratio": 1.4519230769230769, "no_speech_prob": 1.83246451866681e-12}, {"id": 127, "seek": 44040, "start": 454.0, "end": 458.71999999999997, "text": " How does Slickstein justify this mathematically without the machine grinding to a halt?", "tokens": [51045, 1012, 775, 6187, 618, 9089, 20833, 341, 44003, 1553, 264, 3479, 25300, 281, 257, 12479, 30, 51281], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1867101643536542, "compression_ratio": 1.4519230769230769, "no_speech_prob": 1.83246451866681e-12}, {"id": 128, "seek": 45872, "start": 458.72, "end": 462.64000000000004, "text": " It's a very valid architectural concern, I mean, obviously.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 257, 588, 7363, 26621, 3136, 11, 286, 914, 11, 2745, 13, 50561], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 129, "seek": 45872, "start": 463.1, "end": 467.86, "text": " And the answer lies in the distinction between the mathematical model and the physical implementation,", "tokens": [50584, 400, 264, 1867, 9134, 294, 264, 16844, 1296, 264, 18894, 2316, 293, 264, 4001, 11420, 11, 50822], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 130, "seek": 45872, "start": 468.3, "end": 470.84000000000003, "text": " which is bridged by the fourth primitive, collapse.", "tokens": [50844, 597, 307, 16362, 3004, 538, 264, 6409, 28540, 11, 15584, 13, 50971], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 131, "seek": 45872, "start": 471.04, "end": 471.20000000000005, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50981, 1033, 13, 50989], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 132, "seek": 45872, "start": 471.44000000000005, "end": 475.58000000000004, "text": " But strictly looking at refuse for a moment, the refusal is a topological marker.", "tokens": [51001, 583, 20792, 1237, 412, 16791, 337, 257, 1623, 11, 264, 48948, 307, 257, 1192, 4383, 15247, 13, 51208], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 133, "seek": 45872, "start": 475.96000000000004, "end": 477.08000000000004, "text": " It alters the reachability.", "tokens": [51227, 467, 419, 1559, 264, 2524, 2310, 13, 51283], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 134, "seek": 45872, "start": 477.5, "end": 481.02000000000004, "text": " From the perspective of the running program, that data is gone.", "tokens": [51304, 3358, 264, 4585, 295, 264, 2614, 1461, 11, 300, 1412, 307, 2780, 13, 51480], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 135, "seek": 45872, "start": 481.38000000000005, "end": 484.94000000000005, "text": " The fact that it exists in the history is a property of the system's calculus,", "tokens": [51498, 440, 1186, 300, 309, 8198, 294, 264, 2503, 307, 257, 4707, 295, 264, 1185, 311, 33400, 11, 51676], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 136, "seek": 45872, "start": 484.94000000000005, "end": 488.20000000000005, "text": " which guarantees that operations can be reasoned about formally.", "tokens": [51676, 597, 32567, 300, 7705, 393, 312, 1778, 292, 466, 25983, 13, 51839], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17239288270004152, "compression_ratio": 1.670807453416149, "no_speech_prob": 2.0206030858921364e-12}, {"id": 137, "seek": 48820, "start": 488.2, "end": 488.94, "text": " Got it.", "tokens": [50365, 5803, 309, 13, 50402], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 138, "seek": 48820, "start": 489.08, "end": 493.38, "text": " So we have pop as commitment and refuses invalidation without deletion.", "tokens": [50409, 407, 321, 362, 1665, 382, 8371, 293, 33222, 34702, 399, 1553, 1103, 302, 313, 13, 50624], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 139, "seek": 48820, "start": 493.84, "end": 495.56, "text": " Before we get to collapse, let's look at bind.", "tokens": [50647, 4546, 321, 483, 281, 15584, 11, 718, 311, 574, 412, 14786, 13, 50733], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 140, "seek": 48820, "start": 496.03999999999996, "end": 497.59999999999997, "text": " Bind introduces a relationship.", "tokens": [50757, 363, 471, 31472, 257, 2480, 13, 50835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 141, "seek": 48820, "start": 498.12, "end": 502.7, "text": " It couples two elements as dependent without identifying them as the exact same entity.", "tokens": [50861, 467, 20368, 732, 4959, 382, 12334, 1553, 16696, 552, 382, 264, 1900, 912, 13977, 13, 51090], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 142, "seek": 48820, "start": 503.26, "end": 507.65999999999997, "text": " You are taking a value and introducing a new position in the reachable landscape that depends on it.", "tokens": [51118, 509, 366, 1940, 257, 2158, 293, 15424, 257, 777, 2535, 294, 264, 2524, 712, 9661, 300, 5946, 322, 309, 13, 51338], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 143, "seek": 48820, "start": 507.9, "end": 509.48, "text": " It's aliasing, essentially.", "tokens": [51350, 467, 311, 10198, 3349, 11, 4476, 13, 51429], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 144, "seek": 48820, "start": 509.68, "end": 515.64, "text": " I'm creating a new pointer to an existing state rather than duplicating the physical memory footprint of that state.", "tokens": [51439, 286, 478, 4084, 257, 777, 23918, 281, 364, 6741, 1785, 2831, 813, 17154, 990, 264, 4001, 4675, 24222, 295, 300, 1785, 13, 51737], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 145, "seek": 48820, "start": 515.74, "end": 516.96, "text": " Yes, structural sharing.", "tokens": [51742, 1079, 11, 15067, 5414, 13, 51803], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.142311279296875, "compression_ratio": 1.6840390879478828, "no_speech_prob": 1.438460895536453e-12}, {"id": 146, "seek": 51696, "start": 516.96, "end": 523.0600000000001, "text": " It allows the system to reuse historical commitments without violating the append-only rule.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 4045, 264, 1185, 281, 26225, 8584, 26230, 1553, 42201, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 4978, 13, 50670], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13119986961627828, "compression_ratio": 1.5726495726495726, "no_speech_prob": 1.6232487359477132e-12}, {"id": 147, "seek": 51696, "start": 523.22, "end": 524.34, "text": " You don't copy the data.", "tokens": [50678, 509, 500, 380, 5055, 264, 1412, 13, 50734], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13119986961627828, "compression_ratio": 1.5726495726495726, "no_speech_prob": 1.6232487359477132e-12}, {"id": 148, "seek": 51696, "start": 524.72, "end": 527.9200000000001, "text": " You just bind a new reachable position to the old data.", "tokens": [50753, 509, 445, 14786, 257, 777, 2524, 712, 2535, 281, 264, 1331, 1412, 13, 50913], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13119986961627828, "compression_ratio": 1.5726495726495726, "no_speech_prob": 1.6232487359477132e-12}, {"id": 149, "seek": 51696, "start": 528.0400000000001, "end": 532.4200000000001, "text": " Which brings us back to the memory blowout issue and the final primitive, collapse.", "tokens": [50919, 3013, 5607, 505, 646, 281, 264, 4675, 6327, 346, 2734, 293, 264, 2572, 28540, 11, 15584, 13, 51138], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13119986961627828, "compression_ratio": 1.5726495726495726, "no_speech_prob": 1.6232487359477132e-12}, {"id": 150, "seek": 51696, "start": 532.9200000000001, "end": 537.0, "text": " How does collapse save our hardware from drowning in refused history?", "tokens": [51163, 1012, 775, 15584, 3155, 527, 8837, 490, 37198, 294, 14654, 2503, 30, 51367], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13119986961627828, "compression_ratio": 1.5726495726495726, "no_speech_prob": 1.6232487359477132e-12}, {"id": 151, "seek": 51696, "start": 537.38, "end": 539.14, "text": " Well, collapse is an observer operation.", "tokens": [51386, 1042, 11, 15584, 307, 364, 27878, 6916, 13, 51474], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13119986961627828, "compression_ratio": 1.5726495726495726, "no_speech_prob": 1.6232487359477132e-12}, {"id": 152, "seek": 53914, "start": 539.14, "end": 548.72, "text": " It takes the accumulated append-only history of commitments and refusals and it projects them onto a simplified, flattened view based on a specific rule.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 2516, 264, 31346, 34116, 12, 25202, 2503, 295, 26230, 293, 1895, 301, 1124, 293, 309, 4455, 552, 3911, 257, 26335, 11, 24183, 292, 1910, 2361, 322, 257, 2685, 4978, 13, 50844], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1092089017232259, "compression_ratio": 1.5176991150442478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047219093100033e-12}, {"id": 153, "seek": 53914, "start": 549.22, "end": 551.72, "text": " In mathematics, we call this a quotient space.", "tokens": [50869, 682, 18666, 11, 321, 818, 341, 257, 9641, 1196, 1901, 13, 50994], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1092089017232259, "compression_ratio": 1.5176991150442478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047219093100033e-12}, {"id": 154, "seek": 53914, "start": 551.9, "end": 552.8, "text": " A quotient space.", "tokens": [51003, 316, 9641, 1196, 1901, 13, 51048], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1092089017232259, "compression_ratio": 1.5176991150442478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047219093100033e-12}, {"id": 155, "seek": 53914, "start": 553.16, "end": 557.26, "text": " So it's like taking a complex 3D object and looking at its 2D shadow on the wall.", "tokens": [51066, 407, 309, 311, 411, 1940, 257, 3997, 805, 35, 2657, 293, 1237, 412, 1080, 568, 35, 8576, 322, 264, 2929, 13, 51271], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1092089017232259, "compression_ratio": 1.5176991150442478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047219093100033e-12}, {"id": 156, "seek": 53914, "start": 557.52, "end": 559.52, "text": " That's a really great way to visualize it.", "tokens": [51284, 663, 311, 257, 534, 869, 636, 281, 23273, 309, 13, 51384], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1092089017232259, "compression_ratio": 1.5176991150442478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047219093100033e-12}, {"id": 157, "seek": 55952, "start": 559.52, "end": 563.56, "text": " The complexity is still there, but you're only interacting with the flattened projection.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 14024, 307, 920, 456, 11, 457, 291, 434, 787, 18017, 365, 264, 24183, 292, 22743, 13, 50567], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 158, "seek": 55952, "start": 563.9399999999999, "end": 568.34, "text": " Think about event-sourced architectures or how Git version control works.", "tokens": [50586, 6557, 466, 2280, 12, 82, 396, 1232, 6331, 1303, 420, 577, 16939, 3037, 1969, 1985, 13, 50806], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 159, "seek": 55952, "start": 568.38, "end": 568.84, "text": " Oh, yeah.", "tokens": [50808, 876, 11, 1338, 13, 50831], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 160, "seek": 55952, "start": 569.04, "end": 572.3, "text": " In Git, every commit is a permanent part of the history.", "tokens": [50841, 682, 16939, 11, 633, 5599, 307, 257, 10996, 644, 295, 264, 2503, 13, 51004], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 161, "seek": 55952, "start": 572.6999999999999, "end": 574.22, "text": " You never really delete a file.", "tokens": [51024, 509, 1128, 534, 12097, 257, 3991, 13, 51100], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 162, "seek": 55952, "start": 574.36, "end": 577.18, "text": " You just add a new commit that says, hey, this file is removed.", "tokens": [51107, 509, 445, 909, 257, 777, 5599, 300, 1619, 11, 4177, 11, 341, 3991, 307, 7261, 13, 51248], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 163, "seek": 55952, "start": 577.28, "end": 577.54, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51253, 1779, 13, 51266], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18679970961350661, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 1.7237205586495552e-12}, {"id": 164, "seek": 57754, "start": 577.54, "end": 583.64, "text": " The repository history only grows, but when you look at your working directory, you don't see the deleted files.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 25841, 2503, 787, 13156, 11, 457, 562, 291, 574, 412, 428, 1364, 21120, 11, 291, 500, 380, 536, 264, 22981, 7098, 13, 50670], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12620972613899076, "compression_ratio": 1.6735537190082646, "no_speech_prob": 1.912924896610435e-12}, {"id": 165, "seek": 57754, "start": 584.0799999999999, "end": 590.06, "text": " Your working directory is a collapse of the entire tree history into a single, current, usable state.", "tokens": [50692, 2260, 1364, 21120, 307, 257, 15584, 295, 264, 2302, 4230, 2503, 666, 257, 2167, 11, 2190, 11, 29975, 1785, 13, 50991], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12620972613899076, "compression_ratio": 1.6735537190082646, "no_speech_prob": 1.912924896610435e-12}, {"id": 166, "seek": 57754, "start": 590.26, "end": 591.5, "text": " That makes total sense.", "tokens": [51001, 663, 1669, 3217, 2020, 13, 51063], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12620972613899076, "compression_ratio": 1.6735537190082646, "no_speech_prob": 1.912924896610435e-12}, {"id": 167, "seek": 57754, "start": 591.9599999999999, "end": 602.28, "text": " So in the SpherePop machine, the visible data stack, the thing the fourth program actually interacts with, is just a collapse onto an uncollapsed push-and-refuse log.", "tokens": [51086, 407, 294, 264, 318, 6605, 38677, 3479, 11, 264, 8974, 1412, 8630, 11, 264, 551, 264, 6409, 1461, 767, 43582, 365, 11, 307, 445, 257, 15584, 3911, 364, 517, 33891, 2382, 292, 2944, 12, 474, 12, 33115, 438, 3565, 13, 51602], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12620972613899076, "compression_ratio": 1.6735537190082646, "no_speech_prob": 1.912924896610435e-12}, {"id": 168, "seek": 60228, "start": 602.28, "end": 611.72, "text": " Exactly. The rule applied during the collapse is essentially, cancel out every committed pop that has a corresponding refuse layered over it, and expose only the net remaining values.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 440, 4978, 6456, 1830, 264, 15584, 307, 4476, 11, 10373, 484, 633, 7784, 1665, 300, 575, 257, 11760, 16791, 34666, 670, 309, 11, 293, 19219, 787, 264, 2533, 8877, 4190, 13, 50837], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08621808279932072, "compression_ratio": 1.6845238095238095, "no_speech_prob": 1.2112056149704564e-12}, {"id": 169, "seek": 60228, "start": 612.0, "end": 621.98, "text": " From a hardware implementation standpoint, the compiler is free to aggressively garbage collect or overwrite physical memory for anything that has been refused and hidden by a collapse, right?", "tokens": [50851, 3358, 257, 8837, 11420, 15827, 11, 264, 31958, 307, 1737, 281, 32024, 14150, 2500, 420, 670, 21561, 4001, 4675, 337, 1340, 300, 575, 668, 14654, 293, 7633, 538, 257, 15584, 11, 558, 30, 51350], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08621808279932072, "compression_ratio": 1.6845238095238095, "no_speech_prob": 1.2112056149704564e-12}, {"id": 170, "seek": 60228, "start": 622.38, "end": 626.92, "text": " Provided that the program's control flow guarantees it will never need to backtrack to that historical point.", "tokens": [51370, 15685, 2112, 300, 264, 1461, 311, 1969, 3095, 32567, 309, 486, 1128, 643, 281, 646, 19466, 281, 300, 8584, 935, 13, 51597], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08621808279932072, "compression_ratio": 1.6845238095238095, "no_speech_prob": 1.2112056149704564e-12}, {"id": 171, "seek": 60228, "start": 627.36, "end": 631.04, "text": " The append-only timeline is the logical framework that proves the code is safe.", "tokens": [51619, 440, 34116, 12, 25202, 12933, 307, 264, 14978, 8388, 300, 25019, 264, 3089, 307, 3273, 13, 51803], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08621808279932072, "compression_ratio": 1.6845238095238095, "no_speech_prob": 1.2112056149704564e-12}, {"id": 172, "seek": 63104, "start": 631.04, "end": 635.5999999999999, "text": " Once safety is proven, the physical CPU can overwrite registers as usual.", "tokens": [50365, 3443, 4514, 307, 12785, 11, 264, 4001, 13199, 393, 670, 21561, 38351, 382, 7713, 13, 50593], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12053089626764847, "compression_ratio": 1.5833333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.9810559442889897e-12}, {"id": 173, "seek": 63104, "start": 636.02, "end": 638.36, "text": " Wow. Okay. So the privatives are a system of proofs.", "tokens": [50614, 3153, 13, 1033, 13, 407, 264, 2915, 4884, 366, 257, 1185, 295, 8177, 82, 13, 50731], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12053089626764847, "compression_ratio": 1.5833333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.9810559442889897e-12}, {"id": 174, "seek": 63104, "start": 638.9399999999999, "end": 648.38, "text": " Now that we have these four quantum building blocks, pop, refuse, bind, collapse, let's see how they snap together to build the everyday tools a programmer actually uses.", "tokens": [50760, 823, 300, 321, 362, 613, 1451, 13018, 2390, 8474, 11, 1665, 11, 16791, 11, 14786, 11, 15584, 11, 718, 311, 536, 577, 436, 13650, 1214, 281, 1322, 264, 7429, 3873, 257, 32116, 767, 4960, 13, 51232], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12053089626764847, "compression_ratio": 1.5833333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.9810559442889897e-12}, {"id": 175, "seek": 63104, "start": 648.76, "end": 653.4399999999999, "text": " Right. Because the developer isn't out there typing pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.", "tokens": [51251, 1779, 13, 1436, 264, 10754, 1943, 380, 484, 456, 18444, 1665, 11, 16791, 11, 14786, 11, 293, 15584, 13, 51485], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12053089626764847, "compression_ratio": 1.5833333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.9810559442889897e-12}, {"id": 176, "seek": 63104, "start": 653.62, "end": 659.9, "text": " Exactly. They are writing standard stack manipulation words like dup, swap, drop, over, nip.", "tokens": [51494, 7587, 13, 814, 366, 3579, 3832, 8630, 26475, 2283, 411, 274, 1010, 11, 18135, 11, 3270, 11, 670, 11, 297, 647, 13, 51808], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12053089626764847, "compression_ratio": 1.5833333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.9810559442889897e-12}, {"id": 177, "seek": 65990, "start": 659.9, "end": 666.76, "text": " Flinction goes to great lengths to show that every standard fourth stack operation is purely a composite of those four SpherePop primitives.", "tokens": [50365, 3235, 259, 882, 1709, 281, 869, 26329, 281, 855, 300, 633, 3832, 6409, 8630, 6916, 307, 17491, 257, 25557, 295, 729, 1451, 318, 6605, 38677, 2886, 38970, 13, 50708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.121609334592466, "compression_ratio": 1.5471698113207548, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918488315697088e-12}, {"id": 178, "seek": 65990, "start": 667.16, "end": 670.86, "text": " They are just a small algebra of what the paper calls index maps.", "tokens": [50728, 814, 366, 445, 257, 1359, 21989, 295, 437, 264, 3035, 5498, 8186, 11317, 13, 50913], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.121609334592466, "compression_ratio": 1.5471698113207548, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918488315697088e-12}, {"id": 179, "seek": 65990, "start": 671.0799999999999, "end": 676.12, "text": " Let's trace the index maps, starting with dup, which duplicates the top item on the stack.", "tokens": [50924, 961, 311, 13508, 264, 8186, 11317, 11, 2891, 365, 274, 1010, 11, 597, 17154, 1024, 264, 1192, 3174, 322, 264, 8630, 13, 51176], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.121609334592466, "compression_ratio": 1.5471698113207548, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918488315697088e-12}, {"id": 180, "seek": 65990, "start": 676.64, "end": 684.36, "text": " If my stack has a memory address, say 0x4a, and I call dup, I now have two reachable positions pointing to 0x4a.", "tokens": [51202, 759, 452, 8630, 575, 257, 4675, 2985, 11, 584, 1958, 87, 19, 64, 11, 293, 286, 818, 274, 1010, 11, 286, 586, 362, 732, 2524, 712, 8432, 12166, 281, 1958, 87, 19, 64, 13, 51588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.121609334592466, "compression_ratio": 1.5471698113207548, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918488315697088e-12}, {"id": 181, "seek": 68436, "start": 684.36, "end": 693.46, "text": " In SpherePop, dup is heavily reliant on bind. You have a committed position. You use bind to introduce a second position whose value is dependent on the first.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 318, 6605, 38677, 11, 274, 1010, 307, 10950, 1039, 5798, 322, 14786, 13, 509, 362, 257, 7784, 2535, 13, 509, 764, 14786, 281, 5366, 257, 1150, 2535, 6104, 2158, 307, 12334, 322, 264, 700, 13, 50820], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07056780102886731, "compression_ratio": 1.576036866359447, "no_speech_prob": 1.6351719240789708e-12}, {"id": 182, "seek": 68436, "start": 693.62, "end": 693.84, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50828, 1779, 13, 50839], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07056780102886731, "compression_ratio": 1.576036866359447, "no_speech_prob": 1.6351719240789708e-12}, {"id": 183, "seek": 68436, "start": 693.94, "end": 703.04, "text": " Because of bind, they share the exact same physical storage in the underlying hardware, but the calculus treats them as two distinct reachable positions in the quotient space.", "tokens": [50844, 1436, 295, 14786, 11, 436, 2073, 264, 1900, 912, 4001, 6725, 294, 264, 14217, 8837, 11, 457, 264, 33400, 19566, 552, 382, 732, 10644, 2524, 712, 8432, 294, 264, 9641, 1196, 1901, 13, 51299], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07056780102886731, "compression_ratio": 1.576036866359447, "no_speech_prob": 1.6351719240789708e-12}, {"id": 184, "seek": 70304, "start": 703.04, "end": 716.3199999999999, "text": " What about swap? Like if I have value A and value B, and I need to flip them to B and an A, in a naive system, I'd have to move B into a temporary register, move A into B's old spot, and move the temporary register into A's old spot.", "tokens": [50365, 708, 466, 18135, 30, 1743, 498, 286, 362, 2158, 316, 293, 2158, 363, 11, 293, 286, 643, 281, 7929, 552, 281, 363, 293, 364, 316, 11, 294, 257, 29052, 1185, 11, 286, 1116, 362, 281, 1286, 363, 666, 257, 13413, 7280, 11, 1286, 316, 666, 363, 311, 1331, 4008, 11, 293, 1286, 264, 13413, 7280, 666, 316, 311, 1331, 4008, 13, 51029], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07149784088134765, "compression_ratio": 1.6157407407407407, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318014206181177e-12}, {"id": 185, "seek": 70304, "start": 716.76, "end": 719.7199999999999, "text": " It's computationally expensive if the data is large.", "tokens": [51051, 467, 311, 24903, 379, 5124, 498, 264, 1412, 307, 2416, 13, 51199], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07149784088134765, "compression_ratio": 1.6157407407407407, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318014206181177e-12}, {"id": 186, "seek": 70304, "start": 719.8, "end": 722.68, "text": " But in SpherePop, swap doesn't move data at all.", "tokens": [51203, 583, 294, 318, 6605, 38677, 11, 18135, 1177, 380, 1286, 1412, 412, 439, 13, 51347], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07149784088134765, "compression_ratio": 1.6157407407407407, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318014206181177e-12}, {"id": 187, "seek": 70304, "start": 722.92, "end": 723.36, "text": " Wait, really?", "tokens": [51359, 3802, 11, 534, 30, 51381], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07149784088134765, "compression_ratio": 1.6157407407407407, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318014206181177e-12}, {"id": 188, "seek": 72336, "start": 723.36, "end": 730.36, "text": " Yeah. Swap is purely a collapse operation. It takes a pair of reachable positions and observes them under a transposition rule.", "tokens": [50365, 865, 13, 3926, 569, 307, 17491, 257, 15584, 6916, 13, 467, 2516, 257, 6119, 295, 2524, 712, 8432, 293, 3181, 9054, 552, 833, 257, 7132, 5830, 4978, 13, 50715], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08409852981567383, "compression_ratio": 1.6725352112676057, "no_speech_prob": 1.769153942267343e-12}, {"id": 189, "seek": 72336, "start": 730.52, "end": 730.86, "text": " Oh, yeah.", "tokens": [50723, 876, 11, 1338, 13, 50740], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08409852981567383, "compression_ratio": 1.6725352112676057, "no_speech_prob": 1.769153942267343e-12}, {"id": 190, "seek": 72336, "start": 730.96, "end": 741.62, "text": " You are just changing the filter of the quotient space through which the instruction pointer views the history. The underlying append-only log hasn't changed. Your index map just crossed its own pointers.", "tokens": [50745, 509, 366, 445, 4473, 264, 6608, 295, 264, 9641, 1196, 1901, 807, 597, 264, 10951, 23918, 6809, 264, 2503, 13, 440, 14217, 34116, 12, 25202, 3565, 6132, 380, 3105, 13, 2260, 8186, 4471, 445, 14622, 1080, 1065, 44548, 13, 51278], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08409852981567383, "compression_ratio": 1.6725352112676057, "no_speech_prob": 1.769153942267343e-12}, {"id": 191, "seek": 72336, "start": 741.9, "end": 749.02, "text": " That is incredibly elegant. You're altering the topology without moving the payload. Then we have drop, which discards the top item.", "tokens": [51292, 663, 307, 6252, 21117, 13, 509, 434, 11337, 278, 264, 1192, 1793, 1553, 2684, 264, 30918, 13, 1396, 321, 362, 3270, 11, 597, 2983, 2287, 264, 1192, 3174, 13, 51648], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08409852981567383, "compression_ratio": 1.6725352112676057, "no_speech_prob": 1.769153942267343e-12}, {"id": 192, "seek": 74902, "start": 749.02, "end": 757.92, "text": " Drop is the classic refuse followed by collapse. You mark the top position as inadmissible, and then you project a new quotient view that simply omits it.", "tokens": [50365, 17675, 307, 264, 7230, 16791, 6263, 538, 15584, 13, 509, 1491, 264, 1192, 2535, 382, 42148, 76, 41073, 11, 293, 550, 291, 1716, 257, 777, 9641, 1196, 1910, 300, 2935, 3406, 1208, 309, 13, 50810], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07038054305515933, "compression_ratio": 1.5225225225225225, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688837590859596e-12}, {"id": 193, "seek": 74902, "start": 758.4399999999999, "end": 768.76, "text": " Let's get into the slightly more complex permutations, like over. So over takes the second item on the stack and copies it to the top. So if I have A, then B, over results in A, B, A.", "tokens": [50836, 961, 311, 483, 666, 264, 4748, 544, 3997, 4784, 325, 763, 11, 411, 670, 13, 407, 670, 2516, 264, 1150, 3174, 322, 264, 8630, 293, 14341, 309, 281, 264, 1192, 13, 407, 498, 286, 362, 316, 11, 550, 363, 11, 670, 3542, 294, 316, 11, 363, 11, 316, 13, 51352], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07038054305515933, "compression_ratio": 1.5225225225225225, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688837590859596e-12}, {"id": 194, "seek": 76876, "start": 768.76, "end": 773.58, "text": " This is where we start chaining primitives. Over requires duplication and permutation.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 689, 321, 722, 417, 3686, 2886, 38970, 13, 4886, 7029, 17154, 399, 293, 4784, 11380, 13, 50606], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491063081301175, "compression_ratio": 1.58984375, "no_speech_prob": 1.2693307776395768e-12}, {"id": 195, "seek": 76876, "start": 774.0, "end": 774.22, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50627, 1033, 13, 50638], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491063081301175, "compression_ratio": 1.58984375, "no_speech_prob": 1.2693307776395768e-12}, {"id": 196, "seek": 76876, "start": 774.52, "end": 787.52, "text": " First, it uses bind on the second position to create that dependent copy of A. Then it uses collapse with a permutation rule to mathematically shuffle that bound copy to the top of the reachable index map.", "tokens": [50653, 2386, 11, 309, 4960, 14786, 322, 264, 1150, 2535, 281, 1884, 300, 12334, 5055, 295, 316, 13, 1396, 309, 4960, 15584, 365, 257, 4784, 11380, 4978, 281, 44003, 39426, 300, 5472, 5055, 281, 264, 1192, 295, 264, 2524, 712, 8186, 4471, 13, 51303], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491063081301175, "compression_ratio": 1.58984375, "no_speech_prob": 1.2693307776395768e-12}, {"id": 197, "seek": 76876, "start": 787.72, "end": 794.92, "text": " Got it. And nip. Nip is the inverse. It drops the second item but keeps the top one. So A, B becomes just B.", "tokens": [51313, 5803, 309, 13, 400, 297, 647, 13, 426, 647, 307, 264, 17340, 13, 467, 11438, 264, 1150, 3174, 457, 5965, 264, 1192, 472, 13, 407, 316, 11, 363, 3643, 445, 363, 13, 51673], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491063081301175, "compression_ratio": 1.58984375, "no_speech_prob": 1.2693307776395768e-12}, {"id": 198, "seek": 79492, "start": 794.92, "end": 807.5999999999999, "text": " Right. It applies a refuse to position A, marking it inadmissible. Then a collapse is applied to re-index the stack. Position B shifts down to fill the conceptual hole left by the refused A.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 467, 13165, 257, 16791, 281, 2535, 316, 11, 25482, 309, 42148, 76, 41073, 13, 1396, 257, 15584, 307, 6456, 281, 319, 12, 471, 3121, 264, 8630, 13, 29780, 363, 19201, 760, 281, 2836, 264, 24106, 5458, 1411, 538, 264, 14654, 316, 13, 50999], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491951611577248, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.2254827144383973e-12}, {"id": 199, "seek": 79492, "start": 807.74, "end": 818.8399999999999, "text": " The paper makes a really broad algebraic statement about this. It says that if you look at any stack shuffle as an index map, literally just mapping old coordinates to new coordinates, there's an ironclad law.", "tokens": [51006, 440, 3035, 1669, 257, 534, 4152, 21989, 299, 5629, 466, 341, 13, 467, 1619, 300, 498, 291, 574, 412, 604, 8630, 39426, 382, 364, 8186, 4471, 11, 3736, 445, 18350, 1331, 21056, 281, 777, 21056, 11, 456, 311, 364, 6497, 3474, 345, 2101, 13, 51561], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491951611577248, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.2254827144383973e-12}, {"id": 200, "seek": 79492, "start": 819.0, "end": 819.38, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51569, 1079, 13, 51588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09491951611577248, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.2254827144383973e-12}, {"id": 201, "seek": 81938, "start": 819.38, "end": 828.22, "text": " Whenever an index is repeated in your map, it inherently requires a bind. And whenever an index is omitted from your map, it inherently requires a refuse.", "tokens": [50365, 14159, 364, 8186, 307, 10477, 294, 428, 4471, 11, 309, 27993, 7029, 257, 14786, 13, 400, 5699, 364, 8186, 307, 3406, 3944, 490, 428, 4471, 11, 309, 27993, 7029, 257, 16791, 13, 50807], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07500289770273062, "compression_ratio": 1.6505376344086022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2113154446505292e-12}, {"id": 202, "seek": 81938, "start": 828.48, "end": 835.48, "text": " It's a beautiful realization because it proves the primitives are complete. You cannot invent a stack manipulation that falls outside of this framework.", "tokens": [50820, 467, 311, 257, 2238, 25138, 570, 309, 25019, 264, 2886, 38970, 366, 3566, 13, 509, 2644, 7962, 257, 8630, 26475, 300, 8804, 2380, 295, 341, 8388, 13, 51170], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07500289770273062, "compression_ratio": 1.6505376344086022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2113154446505292e-12}, {"id": 203, "seek": 83548, "start": 835.48, "end": 851.04, "text": " It's like a deck of cards. You aren't just shuffling. If you need two aces of spades, you have to formally bind a copy into existence. If you don't want a card anymore, you don't burn it. You refuse to look at it and collapse your hand so it's hidden.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 411, 257, 9341, 295, 5632, 13, 509, 3212, 380, 445, 402, 1245, 1688, 13, 759, 291, 643, 732, 696, 279, 295, 637, 2977, 11, 291, 362, 281, 25983, 14786, 257, 5055, 666, 9123, 13, 759, 291, 500, 380, 528, 257, 2920, 3602, 11, 291, 500, 380, 5064, 309, 13, 509, 16791, 281, 574, 412, 309, 293, 15584, 428, 1011, 370, 309, 311, 7633, 13, 51143], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05144340651375907, "compression_ratio": 1.4764705882352942, "no_speech_prob": 1.6047126735058148e-12}, {"id": 204, "seek": 85104, "start": 851.04, "end": 865.06, "text": " That is a perfect translation. If you need more of something, bind it. If you need less, refuse it. If you are just rearranging, change the collapse rules. It's basically a unified field theory for stack architecture.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 257, 2176, 12853, 13, 759, 291, 643, 544, 295, 746, 11, 14786, 309, 13, 759, 291, 643, 1570, 11, 16791, 309, 13, 759, 291, 366, 445, 29875, 9741, 11, 1319, 264, 15584, 4474, 13, 467, 311, 1936, 257, 26787, 2519, 5261, 337, 8630, 9482, 13, 51066], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08713860605277267, "compression_ratio": 1.4563758389261745, "no_speech_prob": 1.3303595421468306e-12}, {"id": 205, "seek": 86506, "start": 865.06, "end": 874.9, "text": " So we know how to move data around, or rather how our view of the data changes. But where does it actually sit? Let's explore the physical geometry of where these cards live.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 321, 458, 577, 281, 1286, 1412, 926, 11, 420, 2831, 577, 527, 1910, 295, 264, 1412, 2962, 13, 583, 689, 775, 309, 767, 1394, 30, 961, 311, 6839, 264, 4001, 18426, 295, 689, 613, 5632, 1621, 13, 50857], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06941642080034528, "compression_ratio": 1.3082706766917294, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105104887904285e-12}, {"id": 206, "seek": 87490, "start": 874.9, "end": 881.9399999999999, "text": " Right. The paper breaks down four physical ways to implement a data stack. Fixed contiguous, linked, persistent, and segmented.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 440, 3035, 9857, 760, 1451, 4001, 2098, 281, 4445, 257, 1412, 8630, 13, 25538, 292, 660, 30525, 11, 9408, 11, 24315, 11, 293, 9469, 292, 13, 50717], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06978208787979619, "compression_ratio": 1.5982142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.3354459682188291e-12}, {"id": 207, "seek": 87490, "start": 881.98, "end": 883.12, "text": " Let's quickly run through those.", "tokens": [50719, 961, 311, 2661, 1190, 807, 729, 13, 50776], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06978208787979619, "compression_ratio": 1.5982142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.3354459682188291e-12}, {"id": 208, "seek": 87490, "start": 883.22, "end": 896.4599999999999, "text": " These implementations represent the spectrum of how you balance raw speed against the purity of the append-only model. Fixed contiguous is your standard array, a pre-allocated block of memory with a single pointer acting as the stack top.", "tokens": [50781, 1981, 4445, 763, 2906, 264, 11143, 295, 577, 291, 4772, 8936, 3073, 1970, 264, 34382, 295, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 2316, 13, 25538, 292, 660, 30525, 307, 428, 3832, 10225, 11, 257, 659, 12, 336, 905, 770, 3461, 295, 4675, 365, 257, 2167, 23918, 6577, 382, 264, 8630, 1192, 13, 51443], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06978208787979619, "compression_ratio": 1.5982142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.3354459682188291e-12}, {"id": 209, "seek": 87490, "start": 896.72, "end": 899.84, "text": " Which is blazingly fast because of CPU cache locality.", "tokens": [51456, 3013, 307, 16379, 8781, 356, 2370, 570, 295, 13199, 19459, 1628, 1860, 13, 51612], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06978208787979619, "compression_ratio": 1.5982142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.3354459682188291e-12}, {"id": 210, "seek": 87490, "start": 899.84, "end": 904.68, "text": " Yeah, but it completely discards historical states by physically overwriting them.", "tokens": [51612, 865, 11, 457, 309, 2584, 2983, 2287, 8584, 4368, 538, 9762, 670, 19868, 552, 13, 51854], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06978208787979619, "compression_ratio": 1.5982142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.3354459682188291e-12}, {"id": 211, "seek": 90490, "start": 904.9, "end": 910.22, "text": " It violates the append-only rule at the hardware level, even if the software calculus pretends it doesn't.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 3448, 1024, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 4978, 412, 264, 8837, 1496, 11, 754, 498, 264, 4722, 33400, 1162, 2581, 309, 1177, 380, 13, 50631], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06531033251020643, "compression_ratio": 1.5890410958904109, "no_speech_prob": 1.7013941254126475e-12}, {"id": 212, "seek": 90490, "start": 910.3, "end": 918.3, "text": " Exactly. Then you have linked stacks, where every pushed value is a separate heap-allocated node with a pointer to the previous node.", "tokens": [50635, 7587, 13, 1396, 291, 362, 9408, 30792, 11, 689, 633, 9152, 2158, 307, 257, 4994, 33591, 12, 336, 905, 770, 9984, 365, 257, 23918, 281, 264, 3894, 9984, 13, 51035], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06531033251020643, "compression_ratio": 1.5890410958904109, "no_speech_prob": 1.7013941254126475e-12}, {"id": 213, "seek": 90490, "start": 919.0, "end": 928.3, "text": " Infinitely flexible, never runs out of contiguous space, but the pointer dereferencing creates massive cache misses on modern CPUs.", "tokens": [51070, 682, 10925, 11358, 11, 1128, 6676, 484, 295, 660, 30525, 1901, 11, 457, 264, 23918, 15969, 612, 13644, 7829, 5994, 19459, 29394, 322, 4363, 13199, 82, 13, 51535], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06531033251020643, "compression_ratio": 1.5890410958904109, "no_speech_prob": 1.7013941254126475e-12}, {"id": 214, "seek": 90490, "start": 928.3, "end": 933.14, "text": " And segmented stacks are the hybrid, arrays of contiguous memory linked together in chunks.", "tokens": [51535, 400, 9469, 292, 30792, 366, 264, 13051, 11, 41011, 295, 660, 30525, 4675, 9408, 1214, 294, 24004, 13, 51777], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06531033251020643, "compression_ratio": 1.5890410958904109, "no_speech_prob": 1.7013941254126475e-12}, {"id": 215, "seek": 93314, "start": 933.14, "end": 941.92, "text": " Right. But the one Flaccaron focuses on heavily, the one that acts as the literal embodiment of the sphere-pop philosophy, is the persistent stack.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 583, 264, 472, 3235, 326, 6166, 266, 16109, 322, 10950, 11, 264, 472, 300, 10672, 382, 264, 20411, 28935, 2328, 295, 264, 16687, 12, 13872, 10675, 11, 307, 264, 24315, 8630, 13, 50804], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15217569791353666, "compression_ratio": 1.668639053254438, "no_speech_prob": 2.117596896444862e-12}, {"id": 216, "seek": 93314, "start": 942.12, "end": 944.3199999999999, "text": " Okay, walk us through the persistent stack.", "tokens": [50814, 1033, 11, 1792, 505, 807, 264, 24315, 8630, 13, 50924], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15217569791353666, "compression_ratio": 1.668639053254438, "no_speech_prob": 2.117596896444862e-12}, {"id": 217, "seek": 93314, "start": 944.42, "end": 949.38, "text": " The persistent stack is where the hardware model matches the mathematical model perfectly.", "tokens": [50929, 440, 24315, 8630, 307, 689, 264, 8837, 2316, 10676, 264, 18894, 2316, 6239, 13, 51177], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15217569791353666, "compression_ratio": 1.668639053254438, "no_speech_prob": 2.117596896444862e-12}, {"id": 218, "seek": 94938, "start": 949.38, "end": 955.56, "text": " In a persistent stack, when you push a value X onto a stack D, you don't mutate D.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 257, 24315, 8630, 11, 562, 291, 2944, 257, 2158, 1783, 3911, 257, 8630, 413, 11, 291, 500, 380, 5839, 473, 413, 13, 50674], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11207728508191231, "compression_ratio": 1.5360824742268042, "no_speech_prob": 1.5612909185988566e-12}, {"id": 219, "seek": 94938, "start": 955.56, "end": 963.46, "text": " You create a new structural node, node of X and D, which contains your new value and a pointer back to the entirety of the previous stack state.", "tokens": [50674, 509, 1884, 257, 777, 15067, 9984, 11, 9984, 295, 1783, 293, 413, 11, 597, 8306, 428, 777, 2158, 293, 257, 23918, 646, 281, 264, 31557, 295, 264, 3894, 8630, 1785, 13, 51069], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11207728508191231, "compression_ratio": 1.5360824742268042, "no_speech_prob": 1.5612909185988566e-12}, {"id": 220, "seek": 94938, "start": 963.7, "end": 967.36, "text": " Because it points back, older states remain physically reachable.", "tokens": [51081, 1436, 309, 2793, 646, 11, 4906, 4368, 6222, 9762, 2524, 712, 13, 51264], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11207728508191231, "compression_ratio": 1.5360824742268042, "no_speech_prob": 1.5612909185988566e-12}, {"id": 221, "seek": 94938, "start": 967.7, "end": 968.0, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51281, 1079, 13, 51296], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11207728508191231, "compression_ratio": 1.5360824742268042, "no_speech_prob": 1.5612909185988566e-12}, {"id": 222, "seek": 96800, "start": 968.0, "end": 974.74, "text": " So if my control flow branches, and I need to explore two different execution paths, I don't have to copy the entire stack for the second path.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 498, 452, 1969, 3095, 14770, 11, 293, 286, 643, 281, 6839, 732, 819, 15058, 14518, 11, 286, 500, 380, 362, 281, 5055, 264, 2302, 8630, 337, 264, 1150, 3100, 13, 50702], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10380654630407823, "compression_ratio": 1.576158940397351, "no_speech_prob": 2.0048938637745595e-12}, {"id": 223, "seek": 96800, "start": 975.18, "end": 982.06, "text": " No, you don't. Both new branches just push their unique values onto new nodes that share the exact same historical pointer.", "tokens": [50724, 883, 11, 291, 500, 380, 13, 6767, 777, 14770, 445, 2944, 641, 3845, 4190, 3911, 777, 13891, 300, 2073, 264, 1900, 912, 8584, 23918, 13, 51068], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10380654630407823, "compression_ratio": 1.576158940397351, "no_speech_prob": 2.0048938637745595e-12}, {"id": 224, "seek": 96800, "start": 982.32, "end": 987.6, "text": " Structural sharing at its finest. This is the holy grail for speculative execution.", "tokens": [51081, 745, 1757, 1807, 5414, 412, 1080, 28141, 13, 639, 307, 264, 10622, 1295, 388, 337, 49415, 15058, 13, 51345], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10380654630407823, "compression_ratio": 1.576158940397351, "no_speech_prob": 2.0048938637745595e-12}, {"id": 225, "seek": 96800, "start": 988.38, "end": 993.98, "text": " I mean, modern CPUs spend massive amounts of energy guessing which way an IF statement will go.", "tokens": [51384, 286, 914, 11, 4363, 13199, 82, 3496, 5994, 11663, 295, 2281, 17939, 597, 636, 364, 26080, 5629, 486, 352, 13, 51664], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10380654630407823, "compression_ratio": 1.576158940397351, "no_speech_prob": 2.0048938637745595e-12}, {"id": 226, "seek": 96800, "start": 994.1, "end": 995.28, "text": " Oh, yeah. Branch prediction.", "tokens": [51670, 876, 11, 1338, 13, 40482, 17630, 13, 51729], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10380654630407823, "compression_ratio": 1.576158940397351, "no_speech_prob": 2.0048938637745595e-12}, {"id": 227, "seek": 99528, "start": 995.28, "end": 1001.22, "text": " Right. Executing that code before it knows the answer, and then fiercely rolling back all the mutations if it guessed wrong.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 17662, 10861, 300, 3089, 949, 309, 3255, 264, 1867, 11, 293, 550, 16334, 42879, 9439, 646, 439, 264, 29243, 498, 309, 21852, 2085, 13, 50662], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09732641175735829, "compression_ratio": 1.702127659574468, "no_speech_prob": 1.4440987468333777e-12}, {"id": 228, "seek": 99528, "start": 1001.68, "end": 1006.38, "text": " With a persistent stack, rollback is instantaneous. You don't have to unmutate anything.", "tokens": [50685, 2022, 257, 24315, 8630, 11, 3373, 3207, 307, 45596, 13, 509, 500, 380, 362, 281, 19334, 325, 473, 1340, 13, 50920], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09732641175735829, "compression_ratio": 1.702127659574468, "no_speech_prob": 1.4440987468333777e-12}, {"id": 229, "seek": 99528, "start": 1006.5, "end": 1011.38, "text": " Exactly. You just discard the speculative node and reset your instruction pointer back to the historical node.", "tokens": [50926, 7587, 13, 509, 445, 31597, 264, 49415, 9984, 293, 14322, 428, 10951, 23918, 646, 281, 264, 8584, 9984, 13, 51170], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09732641175735829, "compression_ratio": 1.702127659574468, "no_speech_prob": 1.4440987468333777e-12}, {"id": 230, "seek": 99528, "start": 1011.72, "end": 1017.48, "text": " It makes rollback an O of one constant time operation, and it makes transactional memory natively safe.", "tokens": [51187, 467, 1669, 3373, 3207, 364, 422, 295, 472, 5754, 565, 6916, 11, 293, 309, 1669, 46688, 1966, 4675, 8470, 356, 3273, 13, 51475], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09732641175735829, "compression_ratio": 1.702127659574468, "no_speech_prob": 1.4440987468333777e-12}, {"id": 231, "seek": 99528, "start": 1017.9399999999999, "end": 1021.6999999999999, "text": " But the geometry of reachability isn't just about how we build one stack.", "tokens": [51498, 583, 264, 18426, 295, 2524, 2310, 1943, 380, 445, 466, 577, 321, 1322, 472, 8630, 13, 51686], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09732641175735829, "compression_ratio": 1.702127659574468, "no_speech_prob": 1.4440987468333777e-12}, {"id": 232, "seek": 99528, "start": 1021.86, "end": 1024.3, "text": " It's about how we manage different kinds of reachability.", "tokens": [51694, 467, 311, 466, 577, 321, 3067, 819, 3685, 295, 2524, 2310, 13, 51816], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09732641175735829, "compression_ratio": 1.702127659574468, "no_speech_prob": 1.4440987468333777e-12}, {"id": 233, "seek": 102430, "start": 1024.3, "end": 1032.04, "text": " Fourth traditionally separates the data stack from the return stack, and Fliction introduces a third, the control stack.", "tokens": [50365, 23773, 19067, 34149, 264, 1412, 8630, 490, 264, 2736, 8630, 11, 293, 3235, 4105, 31472, 257, 2636, 11, 264, 1969, 8630, 13, 50752], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16362383558943466, "compression_ratio": 1.6984126984126984, "no_speech_prob": 1.415823729956911e-12}, {"id": 234, "seek": 102430, "start": 1032.24, "end": 1037.04, "text": " This separation is vital for system stability. The data stack, D, is the workspace.", "tokens": [50762, 639, 14634, 307, 11707, 337, 1185, 11826, 13, 440, 1412, 8630, 11, 413, 11, 307, 264, 32706, 13, 51002], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16362383558943466, "compression_ratio": 1.6984126984126984, "no_speech_prob": 1.415823729956911e-12}, {"id": 235, "seek": 102430, "start": 1037.58, "end": 1040.48, "text": " It holds the operands, the values, the intermediate calculations.", "tokens": [51029, 467, 9190, 264, 2208, 2967, 11, 264, 4190, 11, 264, 19376, 20448, 13, 51174], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16362383558943466, "compression_ratio": 1.6984126984126984, "no_speech_prob": 1.415823729956911e-12}, {"id": 236, "seek": 102430, "start": 1041.04, "end": 1044.28, "text": " The return stack, R, holds the execution contexts.", "tokens": [51202, 440, 2736, 8630, 11, 497, 11, 9190, 264, 15058, 30628, 13, 51364], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16362383558943466, "compression_ratio": 1.6984126984126984, "no_speech_prob": 1.415823729956911e-12}, {"id": 237, "seek": 104428, "start": 1044.28, "end": 1052.54, "text": " Right. So when a function calls another function, the CPU needs to remember the memory address of where to return once the subroutine finishes.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 407, 562, 257, 2445, 5498, 1071, 2445, 11, 264, 13199, 2203, 281, 1604, 264, 4675, 2985, 295, 689, 281, 2736, 1564, 264, 1422, 81, 45075, 23615, 13, 50778], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12374938920486805, "compression_ratio": 1.5957446808510638, "no_speech_prob": 1.7275621037871058e-12}, {"id": 238, "seek": 104428, "start": 1053.06, "end": 1054.96, "text": " That address lives on the R stack.", "tokens": [50804, 663, 2985, 2909, 322, 264, 497, 8630, 13, 50899], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12374938920486805, "compression_ratio": 1.5957446808510638, "no_speech_prob": 1.7275621037871058e-12}, {"id": 239, "seek": 104428, "start": 1055.22, "end": 1058.86, "text": " And this is where we get into serious security implications in modern computing.", "tokens": [50912, 400, 341, 307, 689, 321, 483, 666, 3156, 3825, 16602, 294, 4363, 15866, 13, 51094], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12374938920486805, "compression_ratio": 1.5957446808510638, "no_speech_prob": 1.7275621037871058e-12}, {"id": 240, "seek": 104428, "start": 1059.06, "end": 1065.32, "text": " Oh, yeah. In languages like C, the data and the return addresses are often smashed together in the same call stack.", "tokens": [51104, 876, 11, 1338, 13, 682, 8650, 411, 383, 11, 264, 1412, 293, 264, 2736, 16862, 366, 2049, 33269, 1214, 294, 264, 912, 818, 8630, 13, 51417], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12374938920486805, "compression_ratio": 1.5957446808510638, "no_speech_prob": 1.7275621037871058e-12}, {"id": 241, "seek": 106532, "start": 1065.32, "end": 1072.72, "text": " If a user inputs a string that is too long, it overflows the data buffer and literally overwrites the return address.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 257, 4195, 15743, 257, 6798, 300, 307, 886, 938, 11, 309, 670, 33229, 264, 1412, 21762, 293, 3736, 670, 86, 30931, 264, 2736, 2985, 13, 50735], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11214586404653695, "compression_ratio": 1.5573122529644268, "no_speech_prob": 1.3147507171504258e-12}, {"id": 242, "seek": 106532, "start": 1072.8, "end": 1077.76, "text": " Right. Which is how return-oriented programming, or ROP attacks, work.", "tokens": [50739, 1779, 13, 3013, 307, 577, 2736, 12, 27414, 9410, 11, 420, 9025, 47, 8122, 11, 589, 13, 50987], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11214586404653695, "compression_ratio": 1.5573122529644268, "no_speech_prob": 1.3147507171504258e-12}, {"id": 243, "seek": 106532, "start": 1078.4399999999998, "end": 1083.1399999999999, "text": " Hackers hijack the control flow by overwriting the R stack with their own malicious addresses.", "tokens": [51021, 35170, 433, 10625, 501, 264, 1969, 3095, 538, 670, 19868, 264, 497, 8630, 365, 641, 1065, 33496, 16862, 13, 51256], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11214586404653695, "compression_ratio": 1.5573122529644268, "no_speech_prob": 1.3147507171504258e-12}, {"id": 244, "seek": 106532, "start": 1083.3799999999999, "end": 1083.72, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51268, 7587, 13, 51285], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11214586404653695, "compression_ratio": 1.5573122529644268, "no_speech_prob": 1.3147507171504258e-12}, {"id": 245, "seek": 106532, "start": 1084.3799999999999, "end": 1088.6799999999998, "text": " Fourth's native dual stack architecture inherently mitigates a massive class of those vulnerabilities", "tokens": [51318, 23773, 311, 8470, 11848, 8630, 9482, 27993, 15699, 1024, 257, 5994, 1508, 295, 729, 37633, 51533], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11214586404653695, "compression_ratio": 1.5573122529644268, "no_speech_prob": 1.3147507171504258e-12}, {"id": 246, "seek": 108868, "start": 1088.68, "end": 1093.8200000000002, "text": " by physically isolating the reachability of data from the reachability of execution flow.", "tokens": [50365, 538, 9762, 48912, 264, 2524, 2310, 295, 1412, 490, 264, 2524, 2310, 295, 15058, 3095, 13, 50622], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 247, "seek": 108868, "start": 1094.16, "end": 1098.44, "text": " The data stack can overflow all day, and it will never touch the instruction pointer's return path.", "tokens": [50639, 440, 1412, 8630, 393, 37772, 439, 786, 11, 293, 309, 486, 1128, 2557, 264, 10951, 23918, 311, 2736, 3100, 13, 50853], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 248, "seek": 108868, "start": 1098.5600000000002, "end": 1099.72, "text": " It's a huge advantage.", "tokens": [50859, 467, 311, 257, 2603, 5002, 13, 50917], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 249, "seek": 108868, "start": 1099.8400000000001, "end": 1103.6000000000001, "text": " Okay, so D is data, R is return flow. What is the control stack? C.", "tokens": [50923, 1033, 11, 370, 413, 307, 1412, 11, 497, 307, 2736, 3095, 13, 708, 307, 264, 1969, 8630, 30, 383, 13, 51111], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 250, "seek": 108868, "start": 1103.6000000000001, "end": 1107.5, "text": " The control stack manages structured loops and complex conditionals.", "tokens": [51111, 440, 1969, 8630, 22489, 18519, 16121, 293, 3997, 4188, 1124, 13, 51306], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 251, "seek": 108868, "start": 1108.0800000000002, "end": 1113.02, "text": " If you have a DO loop in fourth, you need to track the current index, the limit of the loop,", "tokens": [51335, 759, 291, 362, 257, 10699, 6367, 294, 6409, 11, 291, 643, 281, 2837, 264, 2190, 8186, 11, 264, 4948, 295, 264, 6367, 11, 51582], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 252, "seek": 108868, "start": 1113.16, "end": 1116.5, "text": " and the memory address to jump back to at the end of each iteration.", "tokens": [51589, 293, 264, 4675, 2985, 281, 3012, 646, 281, 412, 264, 917, 295, 1184, 24784, 13, 51756], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 253, "seek": 108868, "start": 1116.68, "end": 1116.8600000000001, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51765, 1779, 13, 51774], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14059767104287185, "compression_ratio": 1.698360655737705, "no_speech_prob": 1.586024172238465e-12}, {"id": 254, "seek": 111686, "start": 1116.86, "end": 1121.1799999999998, "text": " That bundle of information is a loop frame, and it lives on the control stack.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 24438, 295, 1589, 307, 257, 6367, 3920, 11, 293, 309, 2909, 322, 264, 1969, 8630, 13, 50581], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 255, "seek": 111686, "start": 1121.4799999999998, "end": 1124.1399999999999, "text": " Now, I noticed a detail in the text I want to ask you about.", "tokens": [50596, 823, 11, 286, 5694, 257, 2607, 294, 264, 2487, 286, 528, 281, 1029, 291, 466, 13, 50729], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 256, "seek": 111686, "start": 1125.1599999999999, "end": 1129.12, "text": " The paper explicitly notes that in practical compiler implementations,", "tokens": [50780, 440, 3035, 20803, 5570, 300, 294, 8496, 31958, 4445, 763, 11, 50978], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 257, "seek": 111686, "start": 1129.52, "end": 1136.3799999999999, "text": " the return stack and the control stack are often multiplexed into the exact same physical memory array.", "tokens": [50998, 264, 2736, 8630, 293, 264, 1969, 8630, 366, 2049, 3311, 2021, 292, 666, 264, 1900, 912, 4001, 4675, 10225, 13, 51341], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 258, "seek": 111686, "start": 1136.78, "end": 1139.74, "text": " They share the same contiguous block of silicon.", "tokens": [51361, 814, 2073, 264, 912, 660, 30525, 3461, 295, 22848, 13, 51509], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 259, "seek": 111686, "start": 1139.82, "end": 1140.56, "text": " They do, yeah.", "tokens": [51513, 814, 360, 11, 1338, 13, 51550], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 260, "seek": 111686, "start": 1140.6999999999998, "end": 1145.4399999999998, "text": " So from an architectural standpoint, why bother separating them conceptually in the calculus?", "tokens": [51557, 407, 490, 364, 26621, 15827, 11, 983, 8677, 29279, 552, 3410, 671, 294, 264, 33400, 30, 51794], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12718889734766506, "compression_ratio": 1.6619718309859155, "no_speech_prob": 1.3940853679783594e-12}, {"id": 261, "seek": 114544, "start": 1145.44, "end": 1148.1200000000001, "text": " If they live in the same house, why pretend they are different?", "tokens": [50365, 759, 436, 1621, 294, 264, 912, 1782, 11, 983, 11865, 436, 366, 819, 30, 50499], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 262, "seek": 114544, "start": 1148.3200000000002, "end": 1153.28, "text": " Because they answer to entirely different admissibility rules regarding their lifespan and collapse mechanics.", "tokens": [50509, 1436, 436, 1867, 281, 7696, 819, 5910, 891, 2841, 4474, 8595, 641, 40361, 293, 15584, 12939, 13, 50757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 263, "seek": 114544, "start": 1153.5800000000002, "end": 1153.76, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50772, 865, 13, 50781], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 264, "seek": 114544, "start": 1153.8400000000001, "end": 1156.46, "text": " Think about the life cycle of a call frame on the return stack.", "tokens": [50785, 6557, 466, 264, 993, 6586, 295, 257, 818, 3920, 322, 264, 2736, 8630, 13, 50916], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 265, "seek": 114544, "start": 1156.56, "end": 1156.7, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50921, 865, 13, 50928], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 266, "seek": 114544, "start": 1156.96, "end": 1162.88, "text": " A function is called, the address is pushed, the function completes, the address is popped, and the program returns.", "tokens": [50941, 316, 2445, 307, 1219, 11, 264, 2985, 307, 9152, 11, 264, 2445, 36362, 11, 264, 2985, 307, 21545, 11, 293, 264, 1461, 11247, 13, 51237], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 267, "seek": 114544, "start": 1163.44, "end": 1167.04, "text": " That frame is discharged by a single final collapse.", "tokens": [51265, 663, 3920, 307, 37081, 538, 257, 2167, 2572, 15584, 13, 51445], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15096374473186455, "compression_ratio": 1.7142857142857142, "no_speech_prob": 1.7624172520686043e-12}, {"id": 268, "seek": 116704, "start": 1167.04, "end": 1168.3999999999999, "text": " It is one and done.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 472, 293, 1096, 13, 50433], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 269, "seek": 116704, "start": 1168.56, "end": 1170.3, "text": " It has a strictly linear lifespan.", "tokens": [50441, 467, 575, 257, 20792, 8213, 40361, 13, 50528], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 270, "seek": 116704, "start": 1170.82, "end": 1171.1, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50554, 1079, 13, 50568], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 271, "seek": 116704, "start": 1171.5, "end": 1174.68, "text": " But a loop frame on the control stack is cyclical.", "tokens": [50588, 583, 257, 6367, 3920, 322, 264, 1969, 8630, 307, 19474, 804, 13, 50747], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 272, "seek": 116704, "start": 1175.32, "end": 1179.2, "text": " It is observed and collapsed repeatedly under an advancing index.", "tokens": [50779, 467, 307, 13095, 293, 24578, 18227, 833, 364, 27267, 8186, 13, 50973], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 273, "seek": 116704, "start": 1179.68, "end": 1180.24, "text": " Ah, I see.", "tokens": [50997, 2438, 11, 286, 536, 13, 51025], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 274, "seek": 116704, "start": 1180.32, "end": 1186.3999999999999, "text": " It survives multiple iterations, managing the state of the repetition, until a specific limit condition is met.", "tokens": [51029, 467, 46231, 3866, 36540, 11, 11642, 264, 1785, 295, 264, 30432, 11, 1826, 257, 2685, 4948, 4188, 307, 1131, 13, 51333], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19067385226865358, "compression_ratio": 1.5025125628140703, "no_speech_prob": 1.404808127464241e-12}, {"id": 275, "seek": 118640, "start": 1186.4, "end": 1193.7800000000002, "text": " So mathematically, a return frame is a linear continuation, while a loop frame is a recursive fixed point.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 44003, 11, 257, 2736, 3920, 307, 257, 8213, 29357, 11, 1339, 257, 6367, 3920, 307, 257, 20560, 488, 6806, 935, 13, 50734], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10476981832626019, "compression_ratio": 1.6313725490196078, "no_speech_prob": 1.495401133651264e-12}, {"id": 276, "seek": 118640, "start": 1193.7800000000002, "end": 1205.8200000000002, "text": " So even if the compiler throws them into the same physical array to save register space, the theoretical model must keep them isolated to prove that a loop won't accidentally be treated as a one-and-done return address.", "tokens": [50734, 407, 754, 498, 264, 31958, 19251, 552, 666, 264, 912, 4001, 10225, 281, 3155, 7280, 1901, 11, 264, 20864, 2316, 1633, 1066, 552, 14621, 281, 7081, 300, 257, 6367, 1582, 380, 15715, 312, 8668, 382, 257, 472, 12, 474, 12, 45939, 2736, 2985, 13, 51336], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10476981832626019, "compression_ratio": 1.6313725490196078, "no_speech_prob": 1.495401133651264e-12}, {"id": 277, "seek": 118640, "start": 1205.96, "end": 1206.42, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51343, 7587, 13, 51366], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10476981832626019, "compression_ratio": 1.6313725490196078, "no_speech_prob": 1.495401133651264e-12}, {"id": 278, "seek": 118640, "start": 1206.98, "end": 1210.64, "text": " If you lose that conceptual distinction, your proofs of reachability fall apart.", "tokens": [51394, 759, 291, 3624, 300, 24106, 16844, 11, 428, 8177, 82, 295, 2524, 2310, 2100, 4936, 13, 51577], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10476981832626019, "compression_ratio": 1.6313725490196078, "no_speech_prob": 1.495401133651264e-12}, {"id": 279, "seek": 121064, "start": 1210.64, "end": 1215.66, "text": " And without those proofs, we can't handle complex control flow on an append-only timeline.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 1553, 729, 8177, 82, 11, 321, 393, 380, 4813, 3997, 1969, 3095, 322, 364, 34116, 12, 25202, 12933, 13, 50616], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 280, "seek": 121064, "start": 1215.8400000000001, "end": 1218.16, "text": " Which brings us to the real temporal magic of the paper.", "tokens": [50625, 3013, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 957, 30881, 5585, 295, 264, 3035, 13, 50741], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 281, "seek": 121064, "start": 1219.0200000000002, "end": 1226.44, "text": " Speaking of loops and suspended execution, how do you handle code that jumps around or repeats if we are in this append-only timeline?", "tokens": [50784, 13069, 295, 16121, 293, 23437, 15058, 11, 577, 360, 291, 4813, 3089, 300, 16704, 926, 420, 35038, 498, 321, 366, 294, 341, 34116, 12, 25202, 12933, 30, 51155], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 282, "seek": 121064, "start": 1226.5, "end": 1226.66, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51158, 1779, 13, 51166], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 283, "seek": 121064, "start": 1226.76, "end": 1227.3000000000002, "text": " Flow control.", "tokens": [51171, 32792, 1969, 13, 51198], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 284, "seek": 121064, "start": 1227.66, "end": 1231.6200000000001, "text": " If we're strictly appending to a history, how do we handle branches and jumps?", "tokens": [51216, 759, 321, 434, 20792, 724, 2029, 281, 257, 2503, 11, 577, 360, 321, 4813, 14770, 293, 16704, 30, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 285, "seek": 121064, "start": 1232.3600000000001, "end": 1235.8200000000002, "text": " When a compiler reads an IF statement, it's reading left to right.", "tokens": [51451, 1133, 257, 31958, 15700, 364, 26080, 5629, 11, 309, 311, 3760, 1411, 281, 558, 13, 51624], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14106697318828212, "compression_ratio": 1.650735294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622649216633701e-12}, {"id": 286, "seek": 123582, "start": 1235.82, "end": 1240.6799999999998, "text": " It hits the word IF, and it knows it needs to jump somewhere if the condition is fresh.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 8664, 264, 1349, 26080, 11, 293, 309, 3255, 309, 2203, 281, 3012, 4079, 498, 264, 4188, 307, 4451, 13, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1248696316247699, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 1.43273077863465e-12}, {"id": 287, "seek": 123582, "start": 1241.4399999999998, "end": 1244.34, "text": " But it hasn't compiled the code inside the block yet.", "tokens": [50646, 583, 309, 6132, 380, 36548, 264, 3089, 1854, 264, 3461, 1939, 13, 50791], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1248696316247699, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 1.43273077863465e-12}, {"id": 288, "seek": 123582, "start": 1244.4399999999998, "end": 1245.5, "text": " It has no idea what's coming.", "tokens": [50796, 467, 575, 572, 1558, 437, 311, 1348, 13, 50849], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1248696316247699, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 1.43273077863465e-12}, {"id": 289, "seek": 123582, "start": 1245.6799999999998, "end": 1245.8799999999999, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50858, 1779, 13, 50868], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1248696316247699, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 1.43273077863465e-12}, {"id": 290, "seek": 123582, "start": 1246.08, "end": 1253.34, "text": " It has no idea how many bytes of machine code that block will take, so you have no idea what memory address the ELSC or then clause will live at.", "tokens": [50878, 467, 575, 572, 1558, 577, 867, 36088, 295, 3479, 3089, 300, 3461, 486, 747, 11, 370, 291, 362, 572, 1558, 437, 4675, 2985, 264, 14426, 20839, 420, 550, 25925, 486, 1621, 412, 13, 51241], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1248696316247699, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 1.43273077863465e-12}, {"id": 291, "seek": 125334, "start": 1253.34, "end": 1258.9599999999998, "text": " The compiler is blind to the future, so it utilizes what Flixion calls deferred continuations.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 31958, 307, 6865, 281, 264, 2027, 11, 370, 309, 4976, 5660, 437, 3235, 970, 313, 5498, 25704, 986, 2993, 763, 13, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13632452487945557, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.489249695785233e-12}, {"id": 292, "seek": 125334, "start": 1259.72, "end": 1266.9199999999998, "text": " When it hits the IF, it emits a branch instruction into the compiled code, but leaves the destination address completely blank.", "tokens": [50684, 1133, 309, 8664, 264, 26080, 11, 309, 846, 1208, 257, 9819, 10951, 666, 264, 36548, 3089, 11, 457, 5510, 264, 12236, 2985, 2584, 8247, 13, 51044], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13632452487945557, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.489249695785233e-12}, {"id": 293, "seek": 125334, "start": 1267.24, "end": 1268.4399999999998, "text": " It creates a placeholder.", "tokens": [51060, 467, 7829, 257, 1081, 20480, 13, 51120], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13632452487945557, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.489249695785233e-12}, {"id": 294, "seek": 125334, "start": 1268.9599999999998, "end": 1274.22, "text": " It's committing a POP to the instruction stream, but the payload of that POP is an unresolved variable.", "tokens": [51146, 467, 311, 26659, 257, 430, 12059, 281, 264, 10951, 4309, 11, 457, 264, 30918, 295, 300, 430, 12059, 307, 364, 517, 495, 29110, 7006, 13, 51409], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13632452487945557, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.489249695785233e-12}, {"id": 295, "seek": 127422, "start": 1274.22, "end": 1278.76, "text": " Yes, and it pushes the memory address of that blank placeholder onto the control stack.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 11, 293, 309, 21020, 264, 4675, 2985, 295, 300, 8247, 1081, 20480, 3911, 264, 1969, 8630, 13, 50592], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12156524133244785, "compression_ratio": 1.6566037735849057, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071201665134426e-12}, {"id": 296, "seek": 127422, "start": 1279.24, "end": 1285.08, "text": " The compiler then continues its monotonic forward march, compiling the body of the IF statement.", "tokens": [50616, 440, 31958, 550, 6515, 1080, 1108, 310, 11630, 2128, 8368, 11, 715, 4883, 264, 1772, 295, 264, 26080, 5629, 13, 50908], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12156524133244785, "compression_ratio": 1.6566037735849057, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071201665134426e-12}, {"id": 297, "seek": 127422, "start": 1285.24, "end": 1285.44, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50916, 1033, 13, 50926], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12156524133244785, "compression_ratio": 1.6566037735849057, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071201665134426e-12}, {"id": 298, "seek": 127422, "start": 1285.68, "end": 1291.7, "text": " When it finally reaches the ELSC or then token, it now knows the exact memory address of the alternate path.", "tokens": [50938, 1133, 309, 2721, 14235, 264, 14426, 20839, 420, 550, 14862, 11, 309, 586, 3255, 264, 1900, 4675, 2985, 295, 264, 18873, 3100, 13, 51239], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12156524133244785, "compression_ratio": 1.6566037735849057, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071201665134426e-12}, {"id": 299, "seek": 127422, "start": 1291.98, "end": 1295.9, "text": " Think of compiling code like laying down railroad tracks while the train is moving.", "tokens": [51253, 6557, 295, 715, 4883, 3089, 411, 14903, 760, 30073, 10218, 1339, 264, 3847, 307, 2684, 13, 51449], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12156524133244785, "compression_ratio": 1.6566037735849057, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071201665134426e-12}, {"id": 300, "seek": 127422, "start": 1296.18, "end": 1299.6200000000001, "text": " When you hit a fork, an IF, you leave a blank signpost.", "tokens": [51463, 1133, 291, 2045, 257, 17716, 11, 364, 26080, 11, 291, 1856, 257, 8247, 1465, 23744, 13, 51635], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12156524133244785, "compression_ratio": 1.6566037735849057, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071201665134426e-12}, {"id": 301, "seek": 129962, "start": 1299.62, "end": 1305.0, "text": " Later, when you know where the track goes, you run back and paint the destination on that signpost.", "tokens": [50365, 11965, 11, 562, 291, 458, 689, 264, 2837, 1709, 11, 291, 1190, 646, 293, 4225, 264, 12236, 322, 300, 1465, 23744, 13, 50634], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 302, "seek": 129962, "start": 1305.52, "end": 1307.3799999999999, "text": " You are literally repairing the future.", "tokens": [50660, 509, 366, 3736, 46158, 264, 2027, 13, 50753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 303, "seek": 129962, "start": 1307.6399999999999, "end": 1308.6799999999998, "text": " That is a brilliant analogy.", "tokens": [50766, 663, 307, 257, 10248, 21663, 13, 50818], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 304, "seek": 129962, "start": 1309.04, "end": 1311.0, "text": " Backpatching or local address repair.", "tokens": [50836, 5833, 79, 29569, 420, 2654, 2985, 10535, 13, 50934], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 305, "seek": 129962, "start": 1311.4799999999998, "end": 1314.9399999999998, "text": " It is monotonic construction followed by targeted repair.", "tokens": [50958, 467, 307, 1108, 310, 11630, 6435, 6263, 538, 15045, 10535, 13, 51131], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 306, "seek": 129962, "start": 1315.58, "end": 1322.28, "text": " But what makes this fascinating in the context of SpherePOP is how it interprets the untaken branch during actual execution.", "tokens": [51163, 583, 437, 1669, 341, 10343, 294, 264, 4319, 295, 318, 6605, 47, 12059, 307, 577, 309, 17489, 1373, 264, 1701, 9846, 9819, 1830, 3539, 15058, 13, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 307, "seek": 129962, "start": 1322.8999999999999, "end": 1325.1599999999999, "text": " Walk me through the calculus of the execution there.", "tokens": [51529, 10818, 385, 807, 264, 33400, 295, 264, 15058, 456, 13, 51642], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1537923992804761, "compression_ratio": 1.6072727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.6110427878898714e-12}, {"id": 308, "seek": 132516, "start": 1325.16, "end": 1332.24, "text": " The paper mathematically encodes a branch as an instance of a choice primitive, POP A or REFUSE B.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 3035, 44003, 2058, 4789, 257, 9819, 382, 364, 5197, 295, 257, 3922, 28540, 11, 430, 12059, 316, 420, 10869, 37, 44626, 363, 13, 50719], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 309, "seek": 132516, "start": 1332.72, "end": 1340.96, "text": " When the compiled code runs and evaluates the condition, if it chooses branch A, it is actively committing to the reachability of A.", "tokens": [50743, 1133, 264, 36548, 3089, 6676, 293, 6133, 1024, 264, 4188, 11, 498, 309, 25963, 9819, 316, 11, 309, 307, 13022, 26659, 281, 264, 2524, 2310, 295, 316, 13, 51155], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 310, "seek": 132516, "start": 1341.2, "end": 1341.8200000000002, "text": " And B?", "tokens": [51167, 400, 363, 30, 51198], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 311, "seek": 132516, "start": 1342.0600000000002, "end": 1343.1200000000001, "text": " B is REFUSE.", "tokens": [51210, 363, 307, 10869, 37, 44626, 13, 51263], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 312, "seek": 132516, "start": 1343.3000000000002, "end": 1345.8200000000002, "text": " It is marked as dynamically inadmissible.", "tokens": [51272, 467, 307, 12658, 382, 43492, 42148, 76, 41073, 13, 51398], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 313, "seek": 132516, "start": 1346.2, "end": 1349.0400000000002, "text": " But remember the core rule, REFUSE does not erase history.", "tokens": [51417, 583, 1604, 264, 4965, 4978, 11, 10869, 37, 44626, 775, 406, 23525, 2503, 13, 51559], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 314, "seek": 132516, "start": 1349.22, "end": 1349.44, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51568, 1779, 13, 51579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17559930390002682, "compression_ratio": 1.502092050209205, "no_speech_prob": 1.9582686167085583e-12}, {"id": 315, "seek": 134944, "start": 1349.44, "end": 1360.6000000000001, "text": " The code for branch B, its unresolved placeholders, its potential state mutations, they remain fundamentally part of the machine's geometric history, even though the instruction pointer cleanly bypassed them.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 3089, 337, 9819, 363, 11, 1080, 517, 495, 29110, 1081, 12916, 11, 1080, 3995, 1785, 29243, 11, 436, 6222, 17879, 644, 295, 264, 3479, 311, 33246, 2503, 11, 754, 1673, 264, 10951, 23918, 2541, 356, 24996, 292, 552, 13, 50923], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10282867326648958, "compression_ratio": 1.6464646464646464, "no_speech_prob": 1.7487006851238385e-12}, {"id": 316, "seek": 134944, "start": 1360.8, "end": 1365.44, "text": " Which, again, ties perfectly into how modern CPU pipelines handle branch prediction.", "tokens": [50933, 3013, 11, 797, 11, 14039, 6239, 666, 577, 4363, 13199, 40168, 4813, 9819, 17630, 13, 51165], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10282867326648958, "compression_ratio": 1.6464646464646464, "no_speech_prob": 1.7487006851238385e-12}, {"id": 317, "seek": 134944, "start": 1366.5800000000002, "end": 1373.54, "text": " The CPU will often fetch and decode the instructions for branch B, just in case, heavily utilizing the cache hierarchy.", "tokens": [51222, 440, 13199, 486, 2049, 23673, 293, 979, 1429, 264, 9415, 337, 9819, 363, 11, 445, 294, 1389, 11, 10950, 26775, 264, 19459, 22333, 13, 51570], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10282867326648958, "compression_ratio": 1.6464646464646464, "no_speech_prob": 1.7487006851238385e-12}, {"id": 318, "seek": 134944, "start": 1374.18, "end": 1378.04, "text": " When it realizes it needs branch A, it flushes the pipeline for B.", "tokens": [51602, 1133, 309, 29316, 309, 2203, 9819, 316, 11, 309, 19568, 279, 264, 15517, 337, 363, 13, 51795], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10282867326648958, "compression_ratio": 1.6464646464646464, "no_speech_prob": 1.7487006851238385e-12}, {"id": 319, "seek": 134944, "start": 1378.16, "end": 1378.76, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51801, 7587, 13, 51831], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10282867326648958, "compression_ratio": 1.6464646464646464, "no_speech_prob": 1.7487006851238385e-12}, {"id": 320, "seek": 137876, "start": 1378.76, "end": 1388.06, "text": " SpherePod provides a mathematical language to describe that flush, not as a destructive deletion, but as a topological refuse that preserves the deterministic safety of the system.", "tokens": [50365, 318, 6605, 40742, 6417, 257, 18894, 2856, 281, 6786, 300, 19568, 11, 406, 382, 257, 26960, 1103, 302, 313, 11, 457, 382, 257, 1192, 4383, 16791, 300, 1183, 9054, 264, 15957, 3142, 4514, 295, 264, 1185, 13, 50830], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 321, "seek": 137876, "start": 1388.54, "end": 1390.42, "text": " That is an excellent hardware parallel.", "tokens": [50854, 663, 307, 364, 7103, 8837, 8952, 13, 50948], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 322, "seek": 137876, "start": 1390.84, "end": 1395.96, "text": " Now, if branching is about deferred continuations, loops are even more mathematically pristine.", "tokens": [50969, 823, 11, 498, 9819, 278, 307, 466, 25704, 986, 2993, 763, 11, 16121, 366, 754, 544, 44003, 582, 42745, 13, 51225], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 323, "seek": 137876, "start": 1396.72, "end": 1402.1, "text": " Flexion shows that you don't actually need a dedicated looping instruction in your primitive set to create a loop.", "tokens": [51263, 29208, 313, 3110, 300, 291, 500, 380, 767, 643, 257, 8374, 6367, 278, 10951, 294, 428, 28540, 992, 281, 1884, 257, 6367, 13, 51532], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 324, "seek": 137876, "start": 1402.22, "end": 1402.98, "text": " How is that possible?", "tokens": [51538, 1012, 307, 300, 1944, 30, 51576], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 325, "seek": 137876, "start": 1403.3, "end": 1406.08, "text": " A CPU physically needs a jump instruction to loop back.", "tokens": [51592, 316, 13199, 9762, 2203, 257, 3012, 10951, 281, 6367, 646, 13, 51731], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 326, "seek": 137876, "start": 1406.22, "end": 1407.68, "text": " At the machine code level, yes.", "tokens": [51738, 1711, 264, 3479, 3089, 1496, 11, 2086, 13, 51811], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1097414812703771, "compression_ratio": 1.654434250764526, "no_speech_prob": 2.4373753883255578e-12}, {"id": 327, "seek": 140768, "start": 1407.68, "end": 1412.3600000000001, "text": " But at the sphere-pup calculus level, a loop is entirely constructed through the bind primitive.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 412, 264, 16687, 12, 79, 1010, 33400, 1496, 11, 257, 6367, 307, 7696, 17083, 807, 264, 14786, 28540, 13, 50599], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 328, "seek": 140768, "start": 1412.8400000000001, "end": 1416.0800000000002, "text": " A loop frame is just a bind of the body's continuation to itself.", "tokens": [50623, 316, 6367, 3920, 307, 445, 257, 14786, 295, 264, 1772, 311, 29357, 281, 2564, 13, 50785], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 329, "seek": 140768, "start": 1416.28, "end": 1417.5600000000002, "text": " Okay, I need to visualize this.", "tokens": [50795, 1033, 11, 286, 643, 281, 23273, 341, 13, 50859], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 330, "seek": 140768, "start": 1418.22, "end": 1421.3600000000001, "text": " A continuation is essentially a pointer to the thing that happens next.", "tokens": [50892, 316, 29357, 307, 4476, 257, 23918, 281, 264, 551, 300, 2314, 958, 13, 51049], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 331, "seek": 140768, "start": 1421.78, "end": 1425.5, "text": " In a linear program, the continuation of line 10 is line 11.", "tokens": [51070, 682, 257, 8213, 1461, 11, 264, 29357, 295, 1622, 1266, 307, 1622, 2975, 13, 51256], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 332, "seek": 140768, "start": 1425.8, "end": 1426.04, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51271, 1779, 13, 51283], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 333, "seek": 140768, "start": 1426.5, "end": 1430.3400000000001, "text": " But in a loop, the thing that happens next is the beginning of the loop itself.", "tokens": [51306, 583, 294, 257, 6367, 11, 264, 551, 300, 2314, 958, 307, 264, 2863, 295, 264, 6367, 2564, 13, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14670215462738612, "compression_ratio": 1.7394957983193278, "no_speech_prob": 1.6879020967378033e-12}, {"id": 334, "seek": 143034, "start": 1430.34, "end": 1433.1399999999999, "text": " Mathematically, it forms a recursive equation.", "tokens": [50365, 15776, 40197, 11, 309, 6422, 257, 20560, 488, 5367, 13, 50505], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19913874501767365, "compression_ratio": 0.9857142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108535946107727e-12}, {"id": 335, "seek": 143034, "start": 1433.76, "end": 1435.3799999999999, "text": " K equals body, then K.", "tokens": [50536, 591, 6915, 1772, 11, 550, 591, 13, 50617], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19913874501767365, "compression_ratio": 0.9857142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108535946107727e-12}, {"id": 336, "seek": 143538, "start": 1435.38, "end": 1436.5600000000002, "text": " So K is the state.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 591, 307, 264, 1785, 13, 50424], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 337, "seek": 143538, "start": 1436.8200000000002, "end": 1439.96, "text": " And the state evaluates the body, and the result is K again.", "tokens": [50437, 400, 264, 1785, 6133, 1024, 264, 1772, 11, 293, 264, 1874, 307, 591, 797, 13, 50594], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 338, "seek": 143538, "start": 1440.1200000000001, "end": 1441.5, "text": " It's a self-referential pointer.", "tokens": [50602, 467, 311, 257, 2698, 12, 265, 612, 2549, 23918, 13, 50671], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 339, "seek": 143538, "start": 1441.72, "end": 1442.18, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50682, 7587, 13, 50705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 340, "seek": 143538, "start": 1442.8000000000002, "end": 1446.92, "text": " You use bind to couple the end of the execution path back to its own origin.", "tokens": [50736, 509, 764, 14786, 281, 1916, 264, 917, 295, 264, 15058, 3100, 646, 281, 1080, 1065, 4957, 13, 50942], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 341, "seek": 143538, "start": 1447.5600000000002, "end": 1450.96, "text": " It's not generating new historical commitments for every iteration.", "tokens": [50974, 467, 311, 406, 17746, 777, 8584, 26230, 337, 633, 24784, 13, 51144], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 342, "seek": 143538, "start": 1451.3200000000002, "end": 1456.9, "text": " It is continuously observing the exact same topological coupling under an advancing index.", "tokens": [51162, 467, 307, 15684, 22107, 264, 1900, 912, 1192, 4383, 37447, 833, 364, 27267, 8186, 13, 51441], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15585928377897842, "compression_ratio": 1.5657894736842106, "no_speech_prob": 1.5858804070303933e-12}, {"id": 343, "seek": 145690, "start": 1456.9, "end": 1465.0800000000002, "text": " And nested loops, like if I have an inner DO loop inside an outer DO loop, how does the append-only timeline unwind them safely?", "tokens": [50365, 400, 15646, 292, 16121, 11, 411, 498, 286, 362, 364, 7284, 10699, 6367, 1854, 364, 10847, 10699, 6367, 11, 577, 775, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 12933, 517, 12199, 552, 11750, 30, 50774], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342226048105771, "compression_ratio": 1.7155963302752293, "no_speech_prob": 1.832453242964216e-12}, {"id": 344, "seek": 145690, "start": 1465.3400000000001, "end": 1467.7, "text": " Nested loops act as nested fixed points.", "tokens": [50787, 426, 21885, 16121, 605, 382, 15646, 292, 6806, 2793, 13, 50905], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342226048105771, "compression_ratio": 1.7155963302752293, "no_speech_prob": 1.832453242964216e-12}, {"id": 345, "seek": 145690, "start": 1468.0800000000002, "end": 1472.8400000000001, "text": " On the control stack, you push the outer loop frame, and then you push the inner loop frame on top of it.", "tokens": [50924, 1282, 264, 1969, 8630, 11, 291, 2944, 264, 10847, 6367, 3920, 11, 293, 550, 291, 2944, 264, 7284, 6367, 3920, 322, 1192, 295, 309, 13, 51162], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342226048105771, "compression_ratio": 1.7155963302752293, "no_speech_prob": 1.832453242964216e-12}, {"id": 346, "seek": 145690, "start": 1472.9, "end": 1476.7, "text": " So the instruction pointer is currently trapped in the recursive bind of the inner frame.", "tokens": [51165, 407, 264, 10951, 23918, 307, 4362, 14994, 294, 264, 20560, 488, 14786, 295, 264, 7284, 3920, 13, 51355], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342226048105771, "compression_ratio": 1.7155963302752293, "no_speech_prob": 1.832453242964216e-12}, {"id": 347, "seek": 145690, "start": 1476.76, "end": 1477.0800000000002, "text": " Correct.", "tokens": [51358, 12753, 13, 51374], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342226048105771, "compression_ratio": 1.7155963302752293, "no_speech_prob": 1.832453242964216e-12}, {"id": 348, "seek": 147708, "start": 1477.08, "end": 1482.4399999999998, "text": " And when the inner loop hits its limit condition, it triggers a collapse that discharges the inner frame.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 562, 264, 7284, 6367, 8664, 1080, 4948, 4188, 11, 309, 22827, 257, 15584, 300, 717, 7374, 2880, 264, 7284, 3920, 13, 50633], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 349, "seek": 147708, "start": 1482.8799999999999, "end": 1488.8999999999999, "text": " Because it's a strict stack geometry, discharging the top frame immediately exposes the frame beneath it.", "tokens": [50655, 1436, 309, 311, 257, 10910, 8630, 18426, 11, 717, 7374, 3249, 264, 1192, 3920, 4258, 1278, 4201, 264, 3920, 17149, 309, 13, 50956], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 350, "seek": 147708, "start": 1488.9399999999998, "end": 1489.58, "text": " The outer frame.", "tokens": [50958, 440, 10847, 3920, 13, 50990], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 351, "seek": 147708, "start": 1489.86, "end": 1490.24, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51004, 1079, 13, 51023], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 352, "seek": 147708, "start": 1490.8799999999999, "end": 1499.1799999999998, "text": " The outer loop's suspended continuation seamlessly wakes up, completely unaffected by the hundreds of iterations that just occurred inside the inner loop,", "tokens": [51055, 440, 10847, 6367, 311, 23437, 29357, 38083, 29610, 493, 11, 2584, 2002, 11259, 292, 538, 264, 6779, 295, 36540, 300, 445, 11068, 1854, 264, 7284, 6367, 11, 51470], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 353, "seek": 147708, "start": 1499.4199999999998, "end": 1502.6, "text": " because the inner loop cleanly collapsed its own local history.", "tokens": [51482, 570, 264, 7284, 6367, 2541, 356, 24578, 1080, 1065, 2654, 2503, 13, 51641], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 354, "seek": 147708, "start": 1502.9199999999998, "end": 1506.22, "text": " It's an incredibly robust way to handle state.", "tokens": [51657, 467, 311, 364, 6252, 13956, 636, 281, 4813, 1785, 13, 51822], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12002560988716457, "compression_ratio": 1.7570422535211268, "no_speech_prob": 1.973801981233758e-12}, {"id": 355, "seek": 150622, "start": 1506.22, "end": 1510.1200000000001, "text": " We are laying a lot of tracks and relying on a lot of signposts.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 366, 14903, 257, 688, 295, 10218, 293, 24140, 322, 257, 688, 295, 1465, 23744, 82, 13, 50560], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 356, "seek": 150622, "start": 1510.42, "end": 1512.46, "text": " But how do we know the train won't crash?", "tokens": [50575, 583, 577, 360, 321, 458, 264, 3847, 1582, 380, 8252, 30, 50677], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 357, "seek": 150622, "start": 1512.92, "end": 1515.68, "text": " How do we know we won't try to pop a value that isn't there?", "tokens": [50700, 1012, 360, 321, 458, 321, 1582, 380, 853, 281, 1665, 257, 2158, 300, 1943, 380, 456, 30, 50838], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 358, "seek": 150622, "start": 1515.98, "end": 1520.66, "text": " Which brings us to the most computationally heavy part of the compiler's job.", "tokens": [50853, 3013, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 881, 24903, 379, 4676, 644, 295, 264, 31958, 311, 1691, 13, 51087], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 359, "seek": 150622, "start": 1521.38, "end": 1523.32, "text": " Static stack effect checking.", "tokens": [51123, 745, 2399, 8630, 1802, 8568, 13, 51220], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 360, "seek": 150622, "start": 1523.82, "end": 1524.98, "text": " The admissibility checker.", "tokens": [51245, 440, 5910, 891, 2841, 1520, 260, 13, 51303], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 361, "seek": 150622, "start": 1525.1200000000001, "end": 1525.24, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51310, 1779, 13, 51316], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 362, "seek": 150622, "start": 1525.58, "end": 1534.08, "text": " Flixion dedicates a significant portion of the paper to proving that you can mathematically guarantee the absence of stack underflows before the code ever runs.", "tokens": [51333, 3235, 970, 313, 37071, 1024, 257, 4776, 8044, 295, 264, 3035, 281, 27221, 300, 291, 393, 44003, 10815, 264, 17145, 295, 8630, 833, 33229, 949, 264, 3089, 1562, 6676, 13, 51758], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 363, "seek": 150622, "start": 1534.08, "end": 1536.0, "text": " This is essentially a type system, right?", "tokens": [51758, 639, 307, 4476, 257, 2010, 1185, 11, 558, 30, 51854], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13608412956123922, "compression_ratio": 1.656957928802589, "no_speech_prob": 2.0522474778600364e-12}, {"id": 364, "seek": 153622, "start": 1536.22, "end": 1541.54, "text": " But instead of checking if a variable is an integer or a string, it's checking the geometric depth of the stack.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 2602, 295, 8568, 498, 257, 7006, 307, 364, 24922, 420, 257, 6798, 11, 309, 311, 8568, 264, 33246, 7161, 295, 264, 8630, 13, 50631], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.129061955672044, "compression_ratio": 1.6218181818181818, "no_speech_prob": 2.0683467959192736e-12}, {"id": 365, "seek": 153622, "start": 1541.72, "end": 1543.14, "text": " It is a structural type system.", "tokens": [50640, 467, 307, 257, 15067, 2010, 1185, 13, 50711], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.129061955672044, "compression_ratio": 1.6218181818181818, "no_speech_prob": 2.0683467959192736e-12}, {"id": 366, "seek": 153622, "start": 1543.3, "end": 1549.48, "text": " Every single word in the fourth dictionary is assigned a mathematical signature that defines its required input and its produced output.", "tokens": [50719, 2048, 2167, 1349, 294, 264, 6409, 25890, 307, 13279, 257, 18894, 13397, 300, 23122, 1080, 4739, 4846, 293, 1080, 7126, 5598, 13, 51028], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.129061955672044, "compression_ratio": 1.6218181818181818, "no_speech_prob": 2.0683467959192736e-12}, {"id": 367, "seek": 153622, "start": 1549.76, "end": 1554.78, "text": " The paper uses the notation alpha times i, arrow alpha times o.", "tokens": [51042, 440, 3035, 4960, 264, 24657, 8961, 1413, 741, 11, 11610, 8961, 1413, 277, 13, 51293], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.129061955672044, "compression_ratio": 1.6218181818181818, "no_speech_prob": 2.0683467959192736e-12}, {"id": 368, "seek": 153622, "start": 1555.92, "end": 1559.38, "text": " Some pack that formula because it's the bedrock of the compiler safety guarantee.", "tokens": [51350, 2188, 2844, 300, 8513, 570, 309, 311, 264, 2901, 17799, 295, 264, 31958, 4514, 10815, 13, 51523], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.129061955672044, "compression_ratio": 1.6218181818181818, "no_speech_prob": 2.0683467959192736e-12}, {"id": 369, "seek": 153622, "start": 1559.56, "end": 1560.3600000000001, "text": " Okay, let's do it.", "tokens": [51532, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 360, 309, 13, 51572], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.129061955672044, "compression_ratio": 1.6218181818181818, "no_speech_prob": 2.0683467959192736e-12}, {"id": 370, "seek": 156036, "start": 1560.36, "end": 1565.34, "text": " If i represents the required input, if the word is addition, i is two discrete items.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 741, 8855, 264, 4739, 4846, 11, 498, 264, 1349, 307, 4500, 11, 741, 307, 732, 27706, 4754, 13, 50614], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2194873264857701, "compression_ratio": 1.3875968992248062, "no_speech_prob": 1.598318808034016e-12}, {"id": 371, "seek": 156036, "start": 1565.3999999999999, "end": 1565.6, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50617, 1779, 13, 50627], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2194873264857701, "compression_ratio": 1.3875968992248062, "no_speech_prob": 1.598318808034016e-12}, {"id": 372, "seek": 156036, "start": 1565.6799999999998, "end": 1567.28, "text": " And o is the produced output.", "tokens": [50631, 400, 277, 307, 264, 7126, 5598, 13, 50711], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2194873264857701, "compression_ratio": 1.3875968992248062, "no_speech_prob": 1.598318808034016e-12}, {"id": 373, "seek": 156036, "start": 1567.28, "end": 1569.9799999999998, "text": " For addition, o is one item, the sum.", "tokens": [50711, 1171, 4500, 11, 277, 307, 472, 3174, 11, 264, 2408, 13, 50846], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2194873264857701, "compression_ratio": 1.3875968992248062, "no_speech_prob": 1.598318808034016e-12}, {"id": 374, "seek": 156036, "start": 1570.6999999999998, "end": 1571.34, "text": " But what is alpha?", "tokens": [50882, 583, 437, 307, 8961, 30, 50914], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2194873264857701, "compression_ratio": 1.3875968992248062, "no_speech_prob": 1.598318808034016e-12}, {"id": 375, "seek": 157134, "start": 1571.34, "end": 1574.4199999999998, "text": " So alpha is the universally quantified prefix.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 8961, 307, 264, 43995, 4426, 2587, 46969, 13, 50519], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 376, "seek": 157134, "start": 1574.86, "end": 1580.3, "text": " It represents the unknown arbitrary depth of the stack beneath the operands you actually care about.", "tokens": [50541, 467, 8855, 264, 9841, 23211, 7161, 295, 264, 8630, 17149, 264, 2208, 2967, 291, 767, 1127, 466, 13, 50813], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 377, "seek": 157134, "start": 1580.3799999999999, "end": 1584.74, "text": " Okay, so if I have a stack with like 500 items on it and I call addition.", "tokens": [50817, 1033, 11, 370, 498, 286, 362, 257, 8630, 365, 411, 5923, 4754, 322, 309, 293, 286, 818, 4500, 13, 51035], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 378, "seek": 157134, "start": 1585.0, "end": 1585.24, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51048, 1779, 13, 51060], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 379, "seek": 157134, "start": 1585.3799999999999, "end": 1588.02, "text": " The addition operation only cares about the top two items.", "tokens": [51067, 440, 4500, 6916, 787, 12310, 466, 264, 1192, 732, 4754, 13, 51199], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 380, "seek": 157134, "start": 1588.12, "end": 1590.6799999999998, "text": " Those bottom 498 items, that's your alpha.", "tokens": [51204, 3950, 2767, 16513, 23, 4754, 11, 300, 311, 428, 8961, 13, 51332], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 381, "seek": 157134, "start": 1590.76, "end": 1591.4599999999998, "text": " Oh, I get it.", "tokens": [51336, 876, 11, 286, 483, 309, 13, 51371], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11995176647020422, "compression_ratio": 1.5495495495495495, "no_speech_prob": 1.6234849836010978e-12}, {"id": 382, "seek": 159146, "start": 1591.46, "end": 1593.8400000000001, "text": " They are topologically shielded from the operation.", "tokens": [50365, 814, 366, 1192, 17157, 10257, 292, 490, 264, 6916, 13, 50484], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15070712713547696, "compression_ratio": 1.599078341013825, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688859274903046e-12}, {"id": 383, "seek": 159146, "start": 1593.94, "end": 1594.5, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [50489, 48746, 736, 13, 50517], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15070712713547696, "compression_ratio": 1.599078341013825, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688859274903046e-12}, {"id": 384, "seek": 159146, "start": 1594.5, "end": 1606.92, "text": " So the signature translates to take a stack of any arbitrary depth, consume the specific top element's i, and leave that exact same arbitrary depth intact while placing the new element's o on top.", "tokens": [50517, 407, 264, 13397, 28468, 281, 747, 257, 8630, 295, 604, 23211, 7161, 11, 14732, 264, 2685, 1192, 4478, 311, 741, 11, 293, 1856, 300, 1900, 912, 23211, 7161, 23493, 1339, 17221, 264, 777, 4478, 311, 277, 322, 1192, 13, 51138], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15070712713547696, "compression_ratio": 1.599078341013825, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688859274903046e-12}, {"id": 385, "seek": 159146, "start": 1607.02, "end": 1610.88, "text": " It guarantees that an operation won't silently corrupt data deep in the stack.", "tokens": [51143, 467, 32567, 300, 364, 6916, 1582, 380, 40087, 17366, 1412, 2452, 294, 264, 8630, 13, 51336], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15070712713547696, "compression_ratio": 1.599078341013825, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688859274903046e-12}, {"id": 386, "seek": 159146, "start": 1611.24, "end": 1611.8400000000001, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51354, 7587, 13, 51384], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15070712713547696, "compression_ratio": 1.599078341013825, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688859274903046e-12}, {"id": 387, "seek": 161184, "start": 1611.84, "end": 1617.0, "text": " And this allows the compiler to perform a unification algorithm during the compilation phase.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 341, 4045, 264, 31958, 281, 2042, 257, 517, 3774, 9284, 1830, 264, 40261, 5574, 13, 50623], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09301828486578805, "compression_ratio": 1.7786259541984732, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732981323984818e-12}, {"id": 388, "seek": 161184, "start": 1617.28, "end": 1628.3, "text": " As it reads your sequence of words left to right, it extracts the signature of each word and attempts to unify the output suffix of the first word with the input prefix of the second word.", "tokens": [50637, 1018, 309, 15700, 428, 8310, 295, 2283, 1411, 281, 558, 11, 309, 8947, 82, 264, 13397, 295, 1184, 1349, 293, 15257, 281, 517, 2505, 264, 5598, 3889, 970, 295, 264, 700, 1349, 365, 264, 4846, 46969, 295, 264, 1150, 1349, 13, 51188], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09301828486578805, "compression_ratio": 1.7786259541984732, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732981323984818e-12}, {"id": 389, "seek": 161184, "start": 1628.56, "end": 1639.3, "text": " So if word A has an output of alpha plus 1, meaning it leaves one item on the stack, and word B has an input requirement of alpha plus 1, it needs one item, they unify perfectly.", "tokens": [51201, 407, 498, 1349, 316, 575, 364, 5598, 295, 8961, 1804, 502, 11, 3620, 309, 5510, 472, 3174, 322, 264, 8630, 11, 293, 1349, 363, 575, 364, 4846, 11695, 295, 8961, 1804, 502, 11, 309, 2203, 472, 3174, 11, 436, 517, 2505, 6239, 13, 51738], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09301828486578805, "compression_ratio": 1.7786259541984732, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732981323984818e-12}, {"id": 390, "seek": 161184, "start": 1639.5, "end": 1639.8, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51748, 1079, 13, 51763], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09301828486578805, "compression_ratio": 1.7786259541984732, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732981323984818e-12}, {"id": 391, "seek": 163980, "start": 1639.8, "end": 1644.3, "text": " This is formalized in the paper as theorem 1, preservation of reachability.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 9860, 1602, 294, 264, 3035, 382, 20904, 502, 11, 27257, 295, 2524, 2310, 13, 50590], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 392, "seek": 163980, "start": 1644.98, "end": 1653.36, "text": " It mathematically proves that if the unification algorithm succeeds for every adjacent pair of words in your program, your program is structurally sound.", "tokens": [50624, 467, 44003, 25019, 300, 498, 264, 517, 3774, 9284, 49263, 337, 633, 24441, 6119, 295, 2283, 294, 428, 1461, 11, 428, 1461, 307, 6594, 6512, 1626, 13, 51043], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 393, "seek": 163980, "start": 1653.76, "end": 1657.78, "text": " It is mathematically impossible for it to suffer a runtime stack underflow.", "tokens": [51063, 467, 307, 44003, 6243, 337, 309, 281, 9753, 257, 34474, 8630, 833, 10565, 13, 51264], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 394, "seek": 163980, "start": 1657.86, "end": 1658.82, "text": " Yes, impossible.", "tokens": [51268, 1079, 11, 6243, 13, 51316], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 395, "seek": 163980, "start": 1659.22, "end": 1661.3, "text": " Let's break this by walking through a failure state.", "tokens": [51336, 961, 311, 1821, 341, 538, 4494, 807, 257, 7763, 1785, 13, 51440], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 396, "seek": 163980, "start": 1661.78, "end": 1663.3, "text": " The paper gives a malformed example.", "tokens": [51464, 440, 3035, 2709, 257, 2806, 22892, 1365, 13, 51540], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 397, "seek": 163980, "start": 1663.6599999999999, "end": 1665.8, "text": " Colon, bad, drop, plus semicolon.", "tokens": [51558, 21408, 11, 1578, 11, 3270, 11, 1804, 27515, 38780, 13, 51665], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 398, "seek": 163980, "start": 1666.62, "end": 1669.06, "text": " Yeah, let's step through the compiler's unification loop for that.", "tokens": [51706, 865, 11, 718, 311, 1823, 807, 264, 31958, 311, 517, 3774, 6367, 337, 300, 13, 51828], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11377941689840178, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489054266612512e-12}, {"id": 399, "seek": 166906, "start": 1669.06, "end": 1671.34, "text": " Okay, what exactly is the compiler doing under the hood?", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 11, 437, 2293, 307, 264, 31958, 884, 833, 264, 13376, 30, 50479], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15285779252836976, "compression_ratio": 1.4805825242718447, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783469587387188e-12}, {"id": 400, "seek": 166906, "start": 1671.6599999999999, "end": 1673.72, "text": " You are defining a new word called bad.", "tokens": [50495, 509, 366, 17827, 257, 777, 1349, 1219, 1578, 13, 50598], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15285779252836976, "compression_ratio": 1.4805825242718447, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783469587387188e-12}, {"id": 401, "seek": 166906, "start": 1674.46, "end": 1676.86, "text": " The compiler parses the first token, drop.", "tokens": [50635, 440, 31958, 21156, 279, 264, 700, 14862, 11, 3270, 13, 50755], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15285779252836976, "compression_ratio": 1.4805825242718447, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783469587387188e-12}, {"id": 402, "seek": 166906, "start": 1677.2, "end": 1679.94, "text": " It looks up drop in the dictionary and fetches its signature.", "tokens": [50772, 467, 1542, 493, 3270, 294, 264, 25890, 293, 15136, 3781, 1080, 13397, 13, 50909], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15285779252836976, "compression_ratio": 1.4805825242718447, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783469587387188e-12}, {"id": 403, "seek": 166906, "start": 1680.6, "end": 1682.56, "text": " Drop takes one item and returns nothing.", "tokens": [50942, 17675, 2516, 472, 3174, 293, 11247, 1825, 13, 51040], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15285779252836976, "compression_ratio": 1.4805825242718447, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783469587387188e-12}, {"id": 404, "seek": 166906, "start": 1683.1799999999998, "end": 1688.22, "text": " So its output state is just alpha, an unknown arbitrary depth.", "tokens": [51071, 407, 1080, 5598, 1785, 307, 445, 8961, 11, 364, 9841, 23211, 7161, 13, 51323], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15285779252836976, "compression_ratio": 1.4805825242718447, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783469587387188e-12}, {"id": 405, "seek": 168822, "start": 1688.22, "end": 1693.78, "text": " Okay, so the compiler updates its internal tracker to note that the current simulated state is alpha.", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 11, 370, 264, 31958, 9205, 1080, 6920, 37516, 281, 3637, 300, 264, 2190, 41713, 1785, 307, 8961, 13, 50643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15527102235075715, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009415151728822e-12}, {"id": 406, "seek": 168822, "start": 1694.5, "end": 1696.4, "text": " Then it parses the next token, plus.", "tokens": [50679, 1396, 309, 21156, 279, 264, 958, 14862, 11, 1804, 13, 50774], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15527102235075715, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009415151728822e-12}, {"id": 407, "seek": 168822, "start": 1696.72, "end": 1697.92, "text": " It looks up plus.", "tokens": [50790, 467, 1542, 493, 1804, 13, 50850], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15527102235075715, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009415151728822e-12}, {"id": 408, "seek": 168822, "start": 1698.48, "end": 1704.1000000000001, "text": " The signature for addition demands an input of exactly two items, so it demands a state of alpha times two.", "tokens": [50878, 440, 13397, 337, 4500, 15107, 364, 4846, 295, 2293, 732, 4754, 11, 370, 309, 15107, 257, 1785, 295, 8961, 1413, 732, 13, 51159], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15527102235075715, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009415151728822e-12}, {"id": 409, "seek": 168822, "start": 1704.28, "end": 1706.06, "text": " And here's where the unification fails.", "tokens": [51168, 400, 510, 311, 689, 264, 517, 3774, 18199, 13, 51257], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15527102235075715, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009415151728822e-12}, {"id": 410, "seek": 170606, "start": 1706.06, "end": 1711.7, "text": " The compiler compares the current simulated state, alpha, with the demanded state, alpha times two.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 31958, 38334, 264, 2190, 41713, 1785, 11, 8961, 11, 365, 264, 28276, 1785, 11, 8961, 1413, 732, 13, 50647], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 411, "seek": 170606, "start": 1711.84, "end": 1712.28, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50654, 7587, 13, 50676], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 412, "seek": 170606, "start": 1712.52, "end": 1716.04, "text": " The compiler's algorithm cannot prove that alpha contains at least two items.", "tokens": [50688, 440, 31958, 311, 9284, 2644, 7081, 300, 8961, 8306, 412, 1935, 732, 4754, 13, 50864], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 413, "seek": 170606, "start": 1716.54, "end": 1717.54, "text": " Maybe the stack is empty.", "tokens": [50889, 2704, 264, 8630, 307, 6707, 13, 50939], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 414, "seek": 170606, "start": 1717.96, "end": 1721.94, "text": " Because the mathematical proof fails, the compiler violently rejects the code.", "tokens": [50960, 1436, 264, 18894, 8177, 18199, 11, 264, 31958, 46728, 8248, 82, 264, 3089, 13, 51159], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 415, "seek": 170606, "start": 1722.12, "end": 1725.0, "text": " So the compiler isn't just translating words to machine code.", "tokens": [51168, 407, 264, 31958, 1943, 380, 445, 35030, 2283, 281, 3479, 3089, 13, 51312], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 416, "seek": 170606, "start": 1725.22, "end": 1728.34, "text": " It's acting as a bouncer, checking everyone's ID at the door.", "tokens": [51323, 467, 311, 6577, 382, 257, 272, 14296, 11, 8568, 1518, 311, 7348, 412, 264, 2853, 13, 51479], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13049621774692727, "compression_ratio": 1.6733870967741935, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758186414418287e-12}, {"id": 417, "seek": 172834, "start": 1728.34, "end": 1729.54, "text": " That's exactly it.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 311, 2293, 309, 13, 50425], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14187419414520264, "compression_ratio": 1.6107142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.617144786136837e-12}, {"id": 418, "seek": 172834, "start": 1729.72, "end": 1737.04, "text": " It is tracking the exact topological trajectory of the machine's memory state by state and rejecting anything that results in an undefined landscape.", "tokens": [50434, 467, 307, 11603, 264, 1900, 1192, 4383, 21512, 295, 264, 3479, 311, 4675, 1785, 538, 1785, 293, 45401, 1340, 300, 3542, 294, 364, 674, 5666, 2001, 9661, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14187419414520264, "compression_ratio": 1.6107142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.617144786136837e-12}, {"id": 419, "seek": 172834, "start": 1737.4599999999998, "end": 1744.58, "text": " And because it does this entirely during compilation, the runtime execution has zero overhead for safety checks.", "tokens": [50821, 400, 570, 309, 775, 341, 7696, 1830, 40261, 11, 264, 34474, 15058, 575, 4018, 19922, 337, 4514, 13834, 13, 51177], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14187419414520264, "compression_ratio": 1.6107142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.617144786136837e-12}, {"id": 420, "seek": 172834, "start": 1744.82, "end": 1747.22, "text": " Which is how Forth achieves its legendary speed.", "tokens": [51189, 3013, 307, 577, 1171, 392, 3538, 977, 1080, 16698, 3073, 13, 51309], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14187419414520264, "compression_ratio": 1.6107142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.617144786136837e-12}, {"id": 421, "seek": 172834, "start": 1747.6399999999999, "end": 1752.62, "text": " The safety is proven statically so the dynamic execution is completely raw and unimpeded.", "tokens": [51330, 440, 4514, 307, 12785, 2219, 984, 370, 264, 8546, 15058, 307, 2584, 8936, 293, 517, 332, 3452, 292, 13, 51579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14187419414520264, "compression_ratio": 1.6107142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.617144786136837e-12}, {"id": 422, "seek": 172834, "start": 1752.78, "end": 1754.24, "text": " So we have all the pieces now.", "tokens": [51587, 407, 321, 362, 439, 264, 3755, 586, 13, 51660], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14187419414520264, "compression_ratio": 1.6107142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.617144786136837e-12}, {"id": 423, "seek": 175424, "start": 1754.24, "end": 1759.06, "text": " We understand the quantum primitives of pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 1223, 264, 13018, 2886, 38970, 295, 1665, 11, 16791, 11, 14786, 11, 293, 15584, 13, 50606], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 424, "seek": 175424, "start": 1759.4, "end": 1762.2, "text": " We've seen how they compose standard stack manipulations.", "tokens": [50623, 492, 600, 1612, 577, 436, 35925, 3832, 8630, 9258, 4136, 13, 50763], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 425, "seek": 175424, "start": 1762.54, "end": 1767.18, "text": " We've explored the stack geometry, branching placeholders, and the mathematical bouncer.", "tokens": [50780, 492, 600, 24016, 264, 8630, 18426, 11, 9819, 278, 1081, 12916, 11, 293, 264, 18894, 272, 14296, 13, 51012], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 426, "seek": 175424, "start": 1767.3, "end": 1767.96, "text": " We have all the pieces.", "tokens": [51018, 492, 362, 439, 264, 3755, 13, 51051], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 427, "seek": 175424, "start": 1768.24, "end": 1771.56, "text": " Let's zoom out and watch the entire compiler run from start to finish.", "tokens": [51065, 961, 311, 8863, 484, 293, 1159, 264, 2302, 31958, 1190, 490, 722, 281, 2413, 13, 51231], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 428, "seek": 175424, "start": 1771.8, "end": 1774.88, "text": " This is where Fliction pulls everything into a unified framework.", "tokens": [51243, 639, 307, 689, 3235, 4105, 16982, 1203, 666, 257, 26787, 8388, 13, 51397], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 429, "seek": 175424, "start": 1775.56, "end": 1779.18, "text": " Most textbooks describe a compiler as a pipeline of distinct passes.", "tokens": [51431, 4534, 33587, 6786, 257, 31958, 382, 257, 15517, 295, 10644, 11335, 13, 51612], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 430, "seek": 175424, "start": 1779.86, "end": 1783.16, "text": " A lexor, a parser, an optimizer, a code generator.", "tokens": [51646, 316, 476, 87, 284, 11, 257, 21156, 260, 11, 364, 5028, 6545, 11, 257, 3089, 19265, 13, 51811], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11852146911621093, "compression_ratio": 1.597444089456869, "no_speech_prob": 1.9427408900346554e-12}, {"id": 431, "seek": 178316, "start": 1783.16, "end": 1784.9, "text": " Treating them as separate software modules.", "tokens": [50365, 20298, 278, 552, 382, 4994, 4722, 16679, 13, 50452], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 432, "seek": 178316, "start": 1785.1200000000001, "end": 1785.24, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50463, 1779, 13, 50469], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 433, "seek": 178316, "start": 1785.68, "end": 1790.78, "text": " But Fliction argues that in the SpherePOP model, a compiler is not a pipeline of modules.", "tokens": [50491, 583, 3235, 4105, 38218, 300, 294, 264, 318, 6605, 47, 12059, 2316, 11, 257, 31958, 307, 406, 257, 15517, 295, 16679, 13, 50746], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 434, "seek": 178316, "start": 1791.16, "end": 1796.3400000000001, "text": " It is simply one continuous, monotonic, growing history of state transformations.", "tokens": [50765, 467, 307, 2935, 472, 10957, 11, 1108, 310, 11630, 11, 4194, 2503, 295, 1785, 34852, 13, 51024], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 435, "seek": 178316, "start": 1797.18, "end": 1801.98, "text": " The paper divides this history into four distinct stages of reachability, from 8.0 to H.3.", "tokens": [51066, 440, 3035, 41347, 341, 2503, 666, 1451, 10644, 10232, 295, 2524, 2310, 11, 490, 1649, 13, 15, 281, 389, 13, 18, 13, 51306], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 436, "seek": 178316, "start": 1802.44, "end": 1803.3200000000002, "text": " Let's trace the continuum.", "tokens": [51329, 961, 311, 13508, 264, 36120, 13, 51373], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 437, "seek": 178316, "start": 1803.8200000000002, "end": 1806.0400000000002, "text": " Stage 1 is 8.0 to H.1 lexing.", "tokens": [51398, 25907, 502, 307, 1649, 13, 15, 281, 389, 13, 16, 476, 87, 278, 13, 51509], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 438, "seek": 178316, "start": 1806.3000000000002, "end": 1806.5800000000002, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51522, 1779, 13, 51536], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 439, "seek": 178316, "start": 1806.8600000000001, "end": 1809.0400000000002, "text": " At 8.0, you just have raw text.", "tokens": [51550, 1711, 1649, 13, 15, 11, 291, 445, 362, 8936, 2487, 13, 51659], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 440, "seek": 178316, "start": 1809.5600000000002, "end": 1810.7, "text": " A stream of characters.", "tokens": [51685, 316, 4309, 295, 4342, 13, 51742], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1931668941791241, "compression_ratio": 1.5631768953068592, "no_speech_prob": 2.624387469493672e-12}, {"id": 441, "seek": 181070, "start": 1810.7, "end": 1815.8400000000001, "text": " As the compiler reads the text, it applies the POP primitive to create symbolic tokens.", "tokens": [50365, 1018, 264, 31958, 15700, 264, 2487, 11, 309, 13165, 264, 430, 12059, 28540, 281, 1884, 25755, 22667, 13, 50622], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 442, "seek": 181070, "start": 1816.28, "end": 1817.22, "text": " It commits to their existence.", "tokens": [50644, 467, 48311, 281, 641, 9123, 13, 50691], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 443, "seek": 181070, "start": 1817.54, "end": 1817.78, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50707, 1079, 13, 50719], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 444, "seek": 181070, "start": 1818.14, "end": 1823.88, "text": " It isolates a cluster of characters, recognizes it as a discrete entity, and commits it to the history.", "tokens": [50737, 467, 7381, 1024, 257, 13630, 295, 4342, 11, 26564, 309, 382, 257, 27706, 13977, 11, 293, 48311, 309, 281, 264, 2503, 13, 51024], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 445, "seek": 181070, "start": 1824.44, "end": 1826.74, "text": " It doesn't know what dupe or 5 means yet.", "tokens": [51052, 467, 1177, 380, 458, 437, 1581, 494, 420, 1025, 1355, 1939, 13, 51167], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 446, "seek": 181070, "start": 1826.96, "end": 1829.6000000000001, "text": " It has just committed to their existence as tokens.", "tokens": [51178, 467, 575, 445, 7784, 281, 641, 9123, 382, 22667, 13, 51310], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 447, "seek": 181070, "start": 1829.72, "end": 1832.8, "text": " It's populating the initial quotient space with raw symbols.", "tokens": [51316, 467, 311, 1665, 12162, 264, 5883, 9641, 1196, 1901, 365, 8936, 16944, 13, 51470], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 448, "seek": 181070, "start": 1833.32, "end": 1837.4, "text": " Then we move to stage 2, H1 to H2, dictionary lookup.", "tokens": [51496, 1396, 321, 1286, 281, 3233, 568, 11, 389, 16, 281, 389, 17, 11, 25890, 574, 1010, 13, 51700], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16704840579275357, "compression_ratio": 1.626865671641791, "no_speech_prob": 1.6043291911974067e-12}, {"id": 449, "seek": 183740, "start": 1837.4, "end": 1839.92, "text": " This stage relies heavily on bind.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 3233, 30910, 10950, 322, 14786, 13, 50491], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 450, "seek": 183740, "start": 1840.3600000000001, "end": 1847.4, "text": " The compiler takes that raw token from H1, searches the system dictionary, and finds the executable code associated with that name.", "tokens": [50513, 440, 31958, 2516, 300, 8936, 14862, 490, 389, 16, 11, 26701, 264, 1185, 25890, 11, 293, 10704, 264, 7568, 712, 3089, 6615, 365, 300, 1315, 13, 50865], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 451, "seek": 183740, "start": 1847.5800000000002, "end": 1850.5, "text": " It then binds the token to its semantic continuation.", "tokens": [50874, 467, 550, 41515, 264, 14862, 281, 1080, 47982, 29357, 13, 51020], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 452, "seek": 183740, "start": 1850.8200000000002, "end": 1855.64, "text": " So now the symbol dupe is inextricably linked to the memory address where the duplication logic lives.", "tokens": [51036, 407, 586, 264, 5986, 1581, 494, 307, 294, 3828, 1341, 1188, 9408, 281, 264, 4675, 2985, 689, 264, 17154, 399, 9952, 2909, 13, 51277], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 453, "seek": 183740, "start": 1855.7, "end": 1856.0800000000002, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51280, 7587, 13, 51299], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 454, "seek": 183740, "start": 1856.2800000000002, "end": 1857.72, "text": " It establishes the semantic coupling.", "tokens": [51309, 467, 8327, 279, 264, 47982, 37447, 13, 51381], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 455, "seek": 183740, "start": 1858.2, "end": 1861.74, "text": " Then we enter stage 3, H2 to H3, code generation.", "tokens": [51405, 1396, 321, 3242, 3233, 805, 11, 389, 17, 281, 389, 18, 11, 3089, 5125, 13, 51582], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 456, "seek": 183740, "start": 1861.8200000000002, "end": 1863.96, "text": " This is where the abstract becomes concrete, right?", "tokens": [51586, 639, 307, 689, 264, 12649, 3643, 9859, 11, 558, 30, 51693], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 457, "seek": 183740, "start": 1864.02, "end": 1864.26, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51696, 1079, 13, 51708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1137587125184106, "compression_ratio": 1.65625, "no_speech_prob": 1.7970127339297925e-12}, {"id": 458, "seek": 186426, "start": 1864.26, "end": 1866.48, "text": " This is where collapse is heavily utilized.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 689, 15584, 307, 10950, 28158, 13, 50476], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11036162376403809, "compression_ratio": 1.6290909090909091, "no_speech_prob": 1.853780367058744e-12}, {"id": 459, "seek": 186426, "start": 1866.96, "end": 1872.32, "text": " The compiler takes all these bound semantic relationships and collapses them into executable machine instructions.", "tokens": [50500, 440, 31958, 2516, 439, 613, 5472, 47982, 6159, 293, 48765, 552, 666, 7568, 712, 3479, 9415, 13, 50768], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11036162376403809, "compression_ratio": 1.6290909090909091, "no_speech_prob": 1.853780367058744e-12}, {"id": 460, "seek": 186426, "start": 1872.54, "end": 1874.3, "text": " What about the deferred branches we talked about?", "tokens": [50779, 708, 466, 264, 25704, 986, 14770, 321, 2825, 466, 30, 50867], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11036162376403809, "compression_ratio": 1.6290909090909091, "no_speech_prob": 1.853780367058744e-12}, {"id": 461, "seek": 186426, "start": 1874.52, "end": 1883.06, "text": " If it encounters a deferred branch, like our IF statement, it leaves it bound as a placeholder, and later collapse performs the backpatching repair.", "tokens": [50878, 759, 309, 26310, 257, 25704, 986, 9819, 11, 411, 527, 26080, 5629, 11, 309, 5510, 309, 5472, 382, 257, 1081, 20480, 11, 293, 1780, 15584, 26213, 264, 646, 79, 29569, 10535, 13, 51305], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11036162376403809, "compression_ratio": 1.6290909090909091, "no_speech_prob": 1.853780367058744e-12}, {"id": 462, "seek": 186426, "start": 1883.54, "end": 1890.16, "text": " The final output, H3, is a strictly admissible, fully resolved block of executable memory.", "tokens": [51329, 440, 2572, 5598, 11, 389, 18, 11, 307, 257, 20792, 5910, 41073, 11, 4498, 20772, 3461, 295, 7568, 712, 4675, 13, 51660], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11036162376403809, "compression_ratio": 1.6290909090909091, "no_speech_prob": 1.853780367058744e-12}, {"id": 463, "seek": 189016, "start": 1890.16, "end": 1895.0800000000002, "text": " So we've gone from text to tokens, tokens as semantic bounds, and bounds to executable code.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 321, 600, 2780, 490, 2487, 281, 22667, 11, 22667, 382, 47982, 29905, 11, 293, 29905, 281, 7568, 712, 3089, 13, 50611], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 464, "seek": 189016, "start": 1895.38, "end": 1896.16, "text": " What is stage 4?", "tokens": [50626, 708, 307, 3233, 1017, 30, 50665], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 465, "seek": 189016, "start": 1896.5400000000002, "end": 1900.1000000000001, "text": " The brilliance of this model is that there is no distinct stage 4.", "tokens": [50684, 440, 8695, 6276, 295, 341, 2316, 307, 300, 456, 307, 572, 10644, 3233, 1017, 13, 50862], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 466, "seek": 189016, "start": 1900.6200000000001, "end": 1902.96, "text": " Execution is simply the replay of H3.", "tokens": [50888, 17662, 1448, 307, 2935, 264, 23836, 295, 389, 18, 13, 51005], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 467, "seek": 189016, "start": 1903.42, "end": 1907.6200000000001, "text": " The running of the program is just the natural continuation of the machine's growing history.", "tokens": [51028, 440, 2614, 295, 264, 1461, 307, 445, 264, 3303, 29357, 295, 264, 3479, 311, 4194, 2503, 13, 51238], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 468, "seek": 189016, "start": 1907.98, "end": 1910.94, "text": " That is an incredibly elegant unified theory.", "tokens": [51256, 663, 307, 364, 6252, 21117, 26787, 5261, 13, 51404], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 469, "seek": 189016, "start": 1911.3400000000001, "end": 1913.8400000000001, "text": " But I want to dig into a major feature of Forth.", "tokens": [51424, 583, 286, 528, 281, 2528, 666, 257, 2563, 4111, 295, 1171, 392, 13, 51549], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 470, "seek": 189016, "start": 1914.3400000000001, "end": 1916.0800000000002, "text": " Forth is famous for being interactive.", "tokens": [51574, 1171, 392, 307, 4618, 337, 885, 15141, 13, 51661], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17689403036366338, "compression_ratio": 1.6309963099630995, "no_speech_prob": 1.335254389694951e-12}, {"id": 471, "seek": 191608, "start": 1916.08, "end": 1919.56, "text": " You can type a command into the terminal, and it executes instantly.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 393, 2010, 257, 5622, 666, 264, 14709, 11, 293, 309, 4454, 1819, 13518, 13, 50539], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 472, "seek": 191608, "start": 1919.86, "end": 1920.48, "text": " Interpret mode.", "tokens": [50554, 5751, 6629, 4391, 13, 50585], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 473, "seek": 191608, "start": 1920.86, "end": 1923.22, "text": " Or you can define a word, and it compiles it for later.", "tokens": [50604, 1610, 291, 393, 6964, 257, 1349, 11, 293, 309, 715, 4680, 309, 337, 1780, 13, 50722], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 474, "seek": 191608, "start": 1923.36, "end": 1924.06, "text": " Compile mode.", "tokens": [50729, 6620, 794, 4391, 13, 50764], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 475, "seek": 191608, "start": 1924.28, "end": 1924.5, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50775, 1779, 13, 50786], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 476, "seek": 191608, "start": 1924.72, "end": 1932.74, "text": " If the compiler is just a monotonic history from H0 to H3, how does it account for the massive behavioral difference between interpreting and compiling?", "tokens": [50797, 759, 264, 31958, 307, 445, 257, 1108, 310, 11630, 2503, 490, 389, 15, 281, 389, 18, 11, 577, 775, 309, 2696, 337, 264, 5994, 19124, 2649, 1296, 37395, 293, 715, 4883, 30, 51198], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 477, "seek": 191608, "start": 1933.1, "end": 1936.3799999999999, "text": " It accounts for it entirely through the timing of the collapse primitive.", "tokens": [51216, 467, 9402, 337, 309, 7696, 807, 264, 10822, 295, 264, 15584, 28540, 13, 51380], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 478, "seek": 191608, "start": 1936.58, "end": 1937.56, "text": " Explain the timing difference.", "tokens": [51390, 39574, 264, 10822, 2649, 13, 51439], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12888284965797706, "compression_ratio": 1.6693227091633467, "no_speech_prob": 1.513263039182311e-12}, {"id": 479, "seek": 193756, "start": 1937.56, "end": 1939.84, "text": " In interpret mode, the system is aggressive.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 7302, 4391, 11, 264, 1185, 307, 10762, 13, 50479], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16070988598991842, "compression_ratio": 1.6653696498054475, "no_speech_prob": 2.1173588056477843e-12}, {"id": 480, "seek": 193756, "start": 1940.62, "end": 1953.3, "text": " The moment the compiler reaches stage 2, the moment it binds a token to its dictionary meaning, it immediately triggers a collapse operation that executes the semantic payload directly against the live machine state.", "tokens": [50518, 440, 1623, 264, 31958, 14235, 3233, 568, 11, 264, 1623, 309, 41515, 257, 14862, 281, 1080, 25890, 3620, 11, 309, 4258, 22827, 257, 15584, 6916, 300, 4454, 1819, 264, 47982, 30918, 3838, 1970, 264, 1621, 3479, 1785, 13, 51152], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16070988598991842, "compression_ratio": 1.6653696498054475, "no_speech_prob": 2.1173588056477843e-12}, {"id": 481, "seek": 193756, "start": 1953.48, "end": 1958.84, "text": " So you T type 5, it binds, it collapses it, and the number 5 is instantly on your data stack.", "tokens": [51161, 407, 291, 314, 2010, 1025, 11, 309, 41515, 11, 309, 48765, 309, 11, 293, 264, 1230, 1025, 307, 13518, 322, 428, 1412, 8630, 13, 51429], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16070988598991842, "compression_ratio": 1.6653696498054475, "no_speech_prob": 2.1173588056477843e-12}, {"id": 482, "seek": 193756, "start": 1959.04, "end": 1959.98, "text": " Immediate execution.", "tokens": [51439, 32157, 473, 15058, 13, 51486], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16070988598991842, "compression_ratio": 1.6653696498054475, "no_speech_prob": 2.1173588056477843e-12}, {"id": 483, "seek": 193756, "start": 1960.3, "end": 1960.56, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51502, 1779, 13, 51515], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16070988598991842, "compression_ratio": 1.6653696498054475, "no_speech_prob": 2.1173588056477843e-12}, {"id": 484, "seek": 193756, "start": 1960.9199999999998, "end": 1963.54, "text": " But in compile mode, the system is deferred.", "tokens": [51533, 583, 294, 31413, 4391, 11, 264, 1185, 307, 25704, 986, 13, 51664], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16070988598991842, "compression_ratio": 1.6653696498054475, "no_speech_prob": 2.1173588056477843e-12}, {"id": 485, "seek": 196354, "start": 1963.54, "end": 1968.32, "text": " When it binds a token to its meaning, it intentionally delays the collapse.", "tokens": [50365, 1133, 309, 41515, 257, 14862, 281, 1080, 3620, 11, 309, 22062, 28610, 264, 15584, 13, 50604], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07460513811432913, "compression_ratio": 1.673913043478261, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688986126557227e-12}, {"id": 486, "seek": 196354, "start": 1968.52, "end": 1968.94, "text": " Oh, I see.", "tokens": [50614, 876, 11, 286, 536, 13, 50635], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07460513811432913, "compression_ratio": 1.673913043478261, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688986126557227e-12}, {"id": 487, "seek": 196354, "start": 1969.06, "end": 1976.1599999999999, "text": " Instead of executing the payload, it appends an execution token, a pointer, into the memory of the word it is actively constructing.", "tokens": [50641, 7156, 295, 32368, 264, 30918, 11, 309, 724, 2581, 364, 15058, 14862, 11, 257, 23918, 11, 666, 264, 4675, 295, 264, 1349, 309, 307, 13022, 39969, 13, 50996], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07460513811432913, "compression_ratio": 1.673913043478261, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688986126557227e-12}, {"id": 488, "seek": 196354, "start": 1976.6399999999999, "end": 1985.3999999999999, "text": " It's just layering more binds into the history, building up the potential energy of H3, saving the execution collapse for whenever the new word is eventually called.", "tokens": [51020, 467, 311, 445, 40754, 544, 41515, 666, 264, 2503, 11, 2390, 493, 264, 3995, 2281, 295, 389, 18, 11, 6816, 264, 15058, 15584, 337, 5699, 264, 777, 1349, 307, 4728, 1219, 13, 51458], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07460513811432913, "compression_ratio": 1.673913043478261, "no_speech_prob": 1.7688986126557227e-12}, {"id": 489, "seek": 198540, "start": 1985.4, "end": 1995.64, "text": " So the entire architectural difference between an interpreter and a compiler boils down to whether the machine decides to collapse the semantic bind immediately or defer it to the output stream.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 264, 2302, 26621, 2649, 1296, 364, 34132, 293, 257, 31958, 35049, 760, 281, 1968, 264, 3479, 14898, 281, 15584, 264, 47982, 14786, 4258, 420, 25704, 309, 281, 264, 5598, 4309, 13, 50877], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08792565334802387, "compression_ratio": 1.6229508196721312, "no_speech_prob": 1.4841313941693635e-12}, {"id": 490, "seek": 198540, "start": 1995.74, "end": 1999.1000000000001, "text": " It's the exact same machinery, just operating with a different topological delay.", "tokens": [50882, 467, 311, 264, 1900, 912, 27302, 11, 445, 7447, 365, 257, 819, 1192, 4383, 8577, 13, 51050], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08792565334802387, "compression_ratio": 1.6229508196721312, "no_speech_prob": 1.4841313941693635e-12}, {"id": 491, "seek": 198540, "start": 1999.22, "end": 1999.5400000000002, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51056, 1079, 13, 51072], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08792565334802387, "compression_ratio": 1.6229508196721312, "no_speech_prob": 1.4841313941693635e-12}, {"id": 492, "seek": 198540, "start": 1999.8200000000002, "end": 2004.44, "text": " And this brings us to the final deep technical mechanism in the paper, threading models.", "tokens": [51086, 400, 341, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 2572, 2452, 6191, 7513, 294, 264, 3035, 11, 7207, 278, 5245, 13, 51317], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08792565334802387, "compression_ratio": 1.6229508196721312, "no_speech_prob": 1.4841313941693635e-12}, {"id": 493, "seek": 198540, "start": 2004.92, "end": 2005.92, "text": " Let's get into threading.", "tokens": [51341, 961, 311, 483, 666, 7207, 278, 13, 51391], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08792565334802387, "compression_ratio": 1.6229508196721312, "no_speech_prob": 1.4841313941693635e-12}, {"id": 494, "seek": 200592, "start": 2005.92, "end": 2011.72, "text": " When we talk about appending execution tokens to a compiled word, we are talking about threading.", "tokens": [50365, 1133, 321, 751, 466, 724, 2029, 15058, 22667, 281, 257, 36548, 1349, 11, 321, 366, 1417, 466, 7207, 278, 13, 50655], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1267057800292969, "compression_ratio": 1.7346153846153847, "no_speech_prob": 1.721449155098198e-12}, {"id": 495, "seek": 200592, "start": 2012.3000000000002, "end": 2016.54, "text": " How does the instruction pointer actually traverse from one compiled word to the next?", "tokens": [50684, 1012, 775, 264, 10951, 23918, 767, 45674, 490, 472, 36548, 1349, 281, 264, 958, 30, 50896], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1267057800292969, "compression_ratio": 1.7346153846153847, "no_speech_prob": 1.721449155098198e-12}, {"id": 496, "seek": 200592, "start": 2017.3200000000002, "end": 2023.1000000000001, "text": " Fliction analyzes indirect threading, direct threading, and subroutine threading.", "tokens": [50935, 3235, 4105, 6459, 12214, 19523, 7207, 278, 11, 2047, 7207, 278, 11, 293, 1422, 81, 45075, 7207, 278, 13, 51224], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1267057800292969, "compression_ratio": 1.7346153846153847, "no_speech_prob": 1.721449155098198e-12}, {"id": 497, "seek": 200592, "start": 2023.4, "end": 2028.3600000000001, "text": " Let's break these down because they have massive implications for CPU performance and cache locality.", "tokens": [51239, 961, 311, 1821, 613, 760, 570, 436, 362, 5994, 16602, 337, 13199, 3389, 293, 19459, 1628, 1860, 13, 51487], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1267057800292969, "compression_ratio": 1.7346153846153847, "no_speech_prob": 1.721449155098198e-12}, {"id": 498, "seek": 200592, "start": 2028.74, "end": 2033.18, "text": " Let's start with indirect threading, which is the classic historical fourth model.", "tokens": [51506, 961, 311, 722, 365, 19523, 7207, 278, 11, 597, 307, 264, 7230, 8584, 6409, 2316, 13, 51728], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1267057800292969, "compression_ratio": 1.7346153846153847, "no_speech_prob": 1.721449155098198e-12}, {"id": 499, "seek": 203318, "start": 2033.18, "end": 2036.66, "text": " In indirect threading, you have an extra layer of indirection.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 19523, 7207, 278, 11, 291, 362, 364, 2857, 4583, 295, 1016, 621, 882, 13, 50539], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08249862307593936, "compression_ratio": 1.8127490039840637, "no_speech_prob": 2.4657938451494443e-12}, {"id": 500, "seek": 203318, "start": 2037.18, "end": 2043.16, "text": " A compiled word is essentially an array of memory addresses, but those addresses don't point directly to machine code.", "tokens": [50565, 316, 36548, 1349, 307, 4476, 364, 10225, 295, 4675, 16862, 11, 457, 729, 16862, 500, 380, 935, 3838, 281, 3479, 3089, 13, 50864], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08249862307593936, "compression_ratio": 1.8127490039840637, "no_speech_prob": 2.4657938451494443e-12}, {"id": 501, "seek": 203318, "start": 2043.5, "end": 2050.54, "text": " They point to an execution token, which is a structural header, and that header contains a pointer to the actual executable machine code.", "tokens": [50881, 814, 935, 281, 364, 15058, 14862, 11, 597, 307, 257, 15067, 23117, 11, 293, 300, 23117, 8306, 257, 23918, 281, 264, 3539, 7568, 712, 3479, 3089, 13, 51233], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08249862307593936, "compression_ratio": 1.8127490039840637, "no_speech_prob": 2.4657938451494443e-12}, {"id": 502, "seek": 203318, "start": 2050.84, "end": 2057.3, "text": " So the CPU has to dereference a pointer, read the address, jump to the header, read another pointer, and then finally jump to the code.", "tokens": [51248, 407, 264, 13199, 575, 281, 15969, 5158, 257, 23918, 11, 1401, 264, 2985, 11, 3012, 281, 264, 23117, 11, 1401, 1071, 23918, 11, 293, 550, 2721, 3012, 281, 264, 3089, 13, 51571], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08249862307593936, "compression_ratio": 1.8127490039840637, "no_speech_prob": 2.4657938451494443e-12}, {"id": 503, "seek": 205730, "start": 2057.3, "end": 2063.34, "text": " That double jump is computationally expensive. It guarantees cache misses on modern processors.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 3834, 3012, 307, 24903, 379, 5124, 13, 467, 32567, 19459, 29394, 322, 4363, 27751, 13, 50667], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 504, "seek": 205730, "start": 2063.82, "end": 2064.2400000000002, "text": " It does.", "tokens": [50691, 467, 775, 13, 50712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 505, "seek": 205730, "start": 2064.6200000000003, "end": 2066.48, "text": " Which is why direct threading was developed.", "tokens": [50731, 3013, 307, 983, 2047, 7207, 278, 390, 4743, 13, 50824], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 506, "seek": 205730, "start": 2066.98, "end": 2068.6200000000003, "text": " Direct threading removes the middleman.", "tokens": [50849, 18308, 7207, 278, 30445, 264, 2808, 1601, 13, 50931], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 507, "seek": 205730, "start": 2069.04, "end": 2072.98, "text": " The compiled array of addresses points straight to the executable machine code fields.", "tokens": [50952, 440, 36548, 10225, 295, 16862, 2793, 2997, 281, 264, 7568, 712, 3479, 3089, 7909, 13, 51149], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 508, "seek": 205730, "start": 2073.32, "end": 2077.28, "text": " It eliminates one dereference cycle, significantly improving performance.", "tokens": [51166, 467, 49893, 472, 15969, 5158, 6586, 11, 10591, 11470, 3389, 13, 51364], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 509, "seek": 205730, "start": 2077.38, "end": 2080.52, "text": " And subroutine threading takes it to the ultimate hardware extreme.", "tokens": [51369, 400, 1422, 81, 45075, 7207, 278, 2516, 309, 281, 264, 9705, 8837, 8084, 13, 51526], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 510, "seek": 205730, "start": 2080.78, "end": 2081.1200000000003, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51539, 1079, 13, 51556], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 511, "seek": 205730, "start": 2081.48, "end": 2085.5800000000004, "text": " In subroutine threading, the compiler abandons the internal array of pointers completely.", "tokens": [51574, 682, 1422, 81, 45075, 7207, 278, 11, 264, 31958, 410, 474, 892, 264, 6920, 10225, 295, 44548, 2584, 13, 51779], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1547793689956013, "compression_ratio": 1.7043189368770764, "no_speech_prob": 1.905457779408093e-12}, {"id": 512, "seek": 208558, "start": 2085.58, "end": 2088.84, "text": " Instead, it emits raw native machine code.", "tokens": [50365, 7156, 11, 309, 846, 1208, 8936, 8470, 3479, 3089, 13, 50528], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 513, "seek": 208558, "start": 2089.2, "end": 2092.54, "text": " It emits native by ASICs or ARM call instructions.", "tokens": [50546, 467, 846, 1208, 8470, 538, 7469, 2532, 82, 420, 45209, 818, 9415, 13, 50713], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 514, "seek": 208558, "start": 2092.92, "end": 2097.8199999999997, "text": " It delegates the control flow entirely to the hardware processor's internal return stack.", "tokens": [50732, 467, 45756, 264, 1969, 3095, 7696, 281, 264, 8837, 15321, 311, 6920, 2736, 8630, 13, 50977], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 515, "seek": 208558, "start": 2098.14, "end": 2103.7, "text": " This is where you get maximum speed, because you are leveraging the silicon's native branch predictors and instruction caches.", "tokens": [50993, 639, 307, 689, 291, 483, 6674, 3073, 11, 570, 291, 366, 32666, 264, 22848, 311, 8470, 9819, 6069, 830, 293, 10951, 269, 13272, 13, 51271], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 516, "seek": 208558, "start": 2104.64, "end": 2110.06, "text": " But why does Flixion dedicate so much space to these threading models in a paper about theoretical primitives?", "tokens": [51318, 583, 983, 775, 3235, 970, 313, 30718, 370, 709, 1901, 281, 613, 7207, 278, 5245, 294, 257, 3035, 466, 20864, 2886, 38970, 30, 51589], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 517, "seek": 208558, "start": 2110.2, "end": 2110.36, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51596, 865, 13, 51604], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 518, "seek": 208558, "start": 2110.56, "end": 2113.42, "text": " What do by-86 call instructions have to do with SpherePop?", "tokens": [51614, 708, 360, 538, 12, 22193, 818, 9415, 362, 281, 360, 365, 318, 6605, 38677, 30, 51757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16275565907106562, "compression_ratio": 1.603960396039604, "no_speech_prob": 2.399293871219177e-12}, {"id": 519, "seek": 211342, "start": 2113.42, "end": 2120.92, "text": " Because Flixion mathematically proves that you do not need different theories to explain these wildly different hardware behaviors.", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 3235, 970, 313, 44003, 25019, 300, 291, 360, 406, 643, 819, 13667, 281, 2903, 613, 34731, 819, 8837, 15501, 13, 50740], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08579914569854737, "compression_ratio": 1.5342465753424657, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283518788188903e-12}, {"id": 520, "seek": 211342, "start": 2121.3, "end": 2132.42, "text": " In the SpherePop calculus, each threading model simply represents a different quantifiable number of reachability transitions between the current instruction pointer and the final semantic transformation.", "tokens": [50759, 682, 264, 318, 6605, 38677, 33400, 11, 1184, 7207, 278, 2316, 2935, 8855, 257, 819, 4426, 30876, 1230, 295, 2524, 2310, 23767, 1296, 264, 2190, 10951, 23918, 293, 264, 2572, 47982, 9887, 13, 51315], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08579914569854737, "compression_ratio": 1.5342465753424657, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283518788188903e-12}, {"id": 521, "seek": 213242, "start": 2132.42, "end": 2140.42, "text": " So indirect threading is just a topological space with one additional POP-like dereference layer built into its structure.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 19523, 7207, 278, 307, 445, 257, 1192, 4383, 1901, 365, 472, 4497, 430, 12059, 12, 4092, 15969, 5158, 4583, 3094, 666, 1080, 3877, 13, 50765], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12730637192726135, "compression_ratio": 1.528497409326425, "no_speech_prob": 1.6423779653981785e-12}, {"id": 522, "seek": 213242, "start": 2141.32, "end": 2145.54, "text": " Direct threading mathematically contracts that layer, collapsing the topology.", "tokens": [50810, 18308, 7207, 278, 44003, 13952, 300, 4583, 11, 45339, 264, 1192, 1793, 13, 51021], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12730637192726135, "compression_ratio": 1.528497409326425, "no_speech_prob": 1.6423779653981785e-12}, {"id": 523, "seek": 213242, "start": 2146.26, "end": 2151.1800000000003, "text": " And subroutine threading delegates the collapse rule directly to the hardware's Michael code.", "tokens": [51057, 400, 1422, 81, 45075, 7207, 278, 45756, 264, 15584, 4978, 3838, 281, 264, 8837, 311, 5116, 3089, 13, 51303], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12730637192726135, "compression_ratio": 1.528497409326425, "no_speech_prob": 1.6423779653981785e-12}, {"id": 524, "seek": 215118, "start": 2151.18, "end": 2159.22, "text": " Exactly. It unifies the gritty, physical realities of hardware implementation with the abstract mathematical purity of the SpherePop model.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 467, 517, 11221, 264, 677, 10016, 11, 4001, 27785, 295, 8837, 11420, 365, 264, 12649, 18894, 34382, 295, 264, 318, 6605, 38677, 2316, 13, 50767], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11452842852391235, "compression_ratio": 1.6566666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 2.3525367851690016e-12}, {"id": 525, "seek": 215118, "start": 2159.58, "end": 2163.1, "text": " It proves that the geometry holds up all the way down to the bare metal.", "tokens": [50785, 467, 25019, 300, 264, 18426, 9190, 493, 439, 264, 636, 760, 281, 264, 6949, 5760, 13, 50961], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11452842852391235, "compression_ratio": 1.6566666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 2.3525367851690016e-12}, {"id": 526, "seek": 215118, "start": 2163.3599999999997, "end": 2167.02, "text": " I have to say, walking through the complete architecture of this paper is staggering.", "tokens": [50974, 286, 362, 281, 584, 11, 4494, 807, 264, 3566, 9482, 295, 341, 3035, 307, 42974, 13, 51157], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11452842852391235, "compression_ratio": 1.6566666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 2.3525367851690016e-12}, {"id": 527, "seek": 215118, "start": 2167.46, "end": 2169.72, "text": " We started with four seemingly simple actions.", "tokens": [51179, 492, 1409, 365, 1451, 18709, 2199, 5909, 13, 51292], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11452842852391235, "compression_ratio": 1.6566666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 2.3525367851690016e-12}, {"id": 528, "seek": 215118, "start": 2170.22, "end": 2177.46, "text": " Commit to a state, mark something as inadmissible without deleting it, couple two states together, and view the history through a flattened projection.", "tokens": [51317, 3046, 270, 281, 257, 1785, 11, 1491, 746, 382, 42148, 76, 41073, 1553, 48946, 309, 11, 1916, 732, 4368, 1214, 11, 293, 1910, 264, 2503, 807, 257, 24183, 292, 22743, 13, 51679], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11452842852391235, "compression_ratio": 1.6566666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 2.3525367851690016e-12}, {"id": 529, "seek": 217746, "start": 2177.46, "end": 2180.06, "text": " Pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.", "tokens": [50365, 10215, 11, 16791, 11, 14786, 11, 293, 15584, 13, 50495], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08991838705660117, "compression_ratio": 1.6474820143884892, "no_speech_prob": 1.5130337304228303e-12}, {"id": 530, "seek": 217746, "start": 2180.42, "end": 2192.6, "text": " Right. And simply by layering those, we mathematically derive memory sharing, instant state rollback, isolated control flow, recursive loops, a static type checker, and a full compiler architecture.", "tokens": [50513, 1779, 13, 400, 2935, 538, 40754, 729, 11, 321, 44003, 28446, 4675, 5414, 11, 9836, 1785, 3373, 3207, 11, 14621, 1969, 3095, 11, 20560, 488, 16121, 11, 257, 13437, 2010, 1520, 260, 11, 293, 257, 1577, 31958, 9482, 13, 51122], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08991838705660117, "compression_ratio": 1.6474820143884892, "no_speech_prob": 1.5130337304228303e-12}, {"id": 531, "seek": 217746, "start": 2192.88, "end": 2201.76, "text": " It is a profound demonstration of how immense, robust complexity can arise organically from a microscopic set of foundational truths.", "tokens": [51136, 467, 307, 257, 14382, 16520, 295, 577, 22920, 11, 13956, 14024, 393, 20288, 1798, 984, 490, 257, 47897, 992, 295, 32195, 30079, 13, 51580], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08991838705660117, "compression_ratio": 1.6474820143884892, "no_speech_prob": 1.5130337304228303e-12}, {"id": 532, "seek": 217746, "start": 2202.06, "end": 2206.92, "text": " You don't need million-line compilers if your foundational physics are mathematically sound.", "tokens": [51595, 509, 500, 380, 643, 2459, 12, 1889, 715, 388, 433, 498, 428, 32195, 10649, 366, 44003, 1626, 13, 51838], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08991838705660117, "compression_ratio": 1.6474820143884892, "no_speech_prob": 1.5130337304228303e-12}, {"id": 533, "seek": 220692, "start": 2206.92, "end": 2209.58, "text": " Which brings us to the ultimate synthesis of the paper.", "tokens": [50365, 3013, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 9705, 30252, 295, 264, 3035, 13, 50498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14777056376139322, "compression_ratio": 1.5675675675675675, "no_speech_prob": 2.176083965799158e-12}, {"id": 534, "seek": 220692, "start": 2209.96, "end": 2216.1800000000003, "text": " If we have to crystallize Fliction's entire thesis into a single defining concept, what is the core takeaway?", "tokens": [50517, 759, 321, 362, 281, 31924, 1125, 3235, 4105, 311, 2302, 22288, 666, 257, 2167, 17827, 3410, 11, 437, 307, 264, 4965, 30681, 30, 50828], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14777056376139322, "compression_ratio": 1.5675675675675675, "no_speech_prob": 2.176083965799158e-12}, {"id": 535, "seek": 220692, "start": 2216.48, "end": 2217.7200000000003, "text": " The paper distills it perfectly.", "tokens": [50843, 440, 3035, 1483, 2565, 309, 6239, 13, 50905], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14777056376139322, "compression_ratio": 1.5675675675675675, "no_speech_prob": 2.176083965799158e-12}, {"id": 536, "seek": 220692, "start": 2218.26, "end": 2221.26, "text": " Fourth compilation is the construction of executable reachability.", "tokens": [50932, 23773, 40261, 307, 264, 6435, 295, 7568, 712, 2524, 2310, 13, 51082], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14777056376139322, "compression_ratio": 1.5675675675675675, "no_speech_prob": 2.176083965799158e-12}, {"id": 537, "seek": 220692, "start": 2221.38, "end": 2222.5, "text": " Executable reachability.", "tokens": [51088, 17662, 32148, 2524, 2310, 13, 51144], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14777056376139322, "compression_ratio": 1.5675675675675675, "no_speech_prob": 2.176083965799158e-12}, {"id": 538, "seek": 222250, "start": 2222.5, "end": 2225.82, "text": " Yes. A variable or a value doesn't exist in a vacuum.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 316, 7006, 420, 257, 2158, 1177, 380, 2514, 294, 257, 14224, 13, 50531], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1520971753704014, "compression_ratio": 1.4860335195530727, "no_speech_prob": 2.2190151192230667e-12}, {"id": 539, "seek": 222250, "start": 2225.98, "end": 2231.18, "text": " It only exists operationally if it occupies a reachable position within the machine's geometric topology.", "tokens": [50539, 467, 787, 8198, 6916, 379, 498, 309, 8073, 530, 257, 2524, 712, 2535, 1951, 264, 3479, 311, 33246, 1192, 1793, 13, 50799], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1520971753704014, "compression_ratio": 1.4860335195530727, "no_speech_prob": 2.2190151192230667e-12}, {"id": 540, "seek": 222250, "start": 2231.72, "end": 2233.6, "text": " A function is not a black box calculation.", "tokens": [50826, 316, 2445, 307, 406, 257, 2211, 2424, 17108, 13, 50920], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1520971753704014, "compression_ratio": 1.4860335195530727, "no_speech_prob": 2.2190151192230667e-12}, {"id": 541, "seek": 222250, "start": 2233.92, "end": 2237.38, "text": " It is a proven, admissible transformation of that reachability.", "tokens": [50936, 467, 307, 257, 12785, 11, 5910, 41073, 9887, 295, 300, 2524, 2310, 13, 51109], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1520971753704014, "compression_ratio": 1.4860335195530727, "no_speech_prob": 2.2190151192230667e-12}, {"id": 542, "seek": 223738, "start": 2237.38, "end": 2241.62, "text": " And underpinning all of it is the law of the append-only timeline.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 833, 17836, 773, 439, 295, 309, 307, 264, 2101, 295, 264, 34116, 12, 25202, 12933, 13, 50577], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13239743159367487, "compression_ratio": 1.5627705627705628, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279284557011887e-12}, {"id": 543, "seek": 223738, "start": 2242.82, "end": 2245.78, "text": " A value only exists if it is reachable on a stack.", "tokens": [50637, 316, 2158, 787, 8198, 498, 309, 307, 2524, 712, 322, 257, 8630, 13, 50785], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13239743159367487, "compression_ratio": 1.5627705627705628, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279284557011887e-12}, {"id": 544, "seek": 223738, "start": 2246.38, "end": 2248.12, "text": " Nothing is ever truly erased.", "tokens": [50815, 6693, 307, 1562, 4908, 38359, 13, 50902], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13239743159367487, "compression_ratio": 1.5627705627705628, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279284557011887e-12}, {"id": 545, "seek": 223738, "start": 2248.6400000000003, "end": 2257.0, "text": " The illusion of mutability and deletion is just a sequence of refusals layered over an intact history, waiting to be collapsed from our immediate view.", "tokens": [50928, 440, 18854, 295, 5839, 2310, 293, 1103, 302, 313, 307, 445, 257, 8310, 295, 1895, 301, 1124, 34666, 670, 364, 23493, 2503, 11, 3806, 281, 312, 24578, 490, 527, 11629, 1910, 13, 51346], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13239743159367487, "compression_ratio": 1.5627705627705628, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279284557011887e-12}, {"id": 546, "seek": 223738, "start": 2257.26, "end": 2260.56, "text": " That is the architectural and philosophical core of the work.", "tokens": [51359, 663, 307, 264, 26621, 293, 25066, 4965, 295, 264, 589, 13, 51524], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13239743159367487, "compression_ratio": 1.5627705627705628, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279284557011887e-12}, {"id": 547, "seek": 226056, "start": 2260.56, "end": 2262.08, "text": " And that leads me to a final thought.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 300, 6689, 385, 281, 257, 2572, 1194, 13, 50441], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14123320815586807, "compression_ratio": 1.564748201438849, "no_speech_prob": 2.2894312314003518e-12}, {"id": 548, "seek": 226056, "start": 2262.58, "end": 2274.14, "text": " Moving away from compiler theory and looking at software architecture as a whole, if we take this sphere-pop reality that true deletion is a mathematical fiction, how does that alter the way we build everyday systems?", "tokens": [50466, 14242, 1314, 490, 31958, 5261, 293, 1237, 412, 4722, 9482, 382, 257, 1379, 11, 498, 321, 747, 341, 16687, 12, 13872, 4103, 300, 2074, 1103, 302, 313, 307, 257, 18894, 13266, 11, 577, 775, 300, 11337, 264, 636, 321, 1322, 7429, 3652, 30, 51044], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14123320815586807, "compression_ratio": 1.564748201438849, "no_speech_prob": 2.2894312314003518e-12}, {"id": 549, "seek": 226056, "start": 2274.32, "end": 2276.82, "text": " It challenges the very foundation of CRUD apps.", "tokens": [51053, 467, 4759, 264, 588, 7030, 295, 14123, 9438, 7733, 13, 51178], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14123320815586807, "compression_ratio": 1.564748201438849, "no_speech_prob": 2.2894312314003518e-12}, {"id": 550, "seek": 226056, "start": 2277.32, "end": 2279.38, "text": " Create, read, update, delete.", "tokens": [51203, 20248, 11, 1401, 11, 5623, 11, 12097, 13, 51306], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14123320815586807, "compression_ratio": 1.564748201438849, "no_speech_prob": 2.2894312314003518e-12}, {"id": 551, "seek": 226056, "start": 2279.58, "end": 2279.96, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51316, 7587, 13, 51335], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14123320815586807, "compression_ratio": 1.564748201438849, "no_speech_prob": 2.2894312314003518e-12}, {"id": 552, "seek": 226056, "start": 2280.4, "end": 2284.56, "text": " We spend so much engineering effort building fragile systems that constantly overwrite data.", "tokens": [51357, 492, 3496, 370, 709, 7043, 4630, 2390, 23847, 3652, 300, 6460, 670, 21561, 1412, 13, 51565], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14123320815586807, "compression_ratio": 1.564748201438849, "no_speech_prob": 2.2894312314003518e-12}, {"id": 553, "seek": 228456, "start": 2284.56, "end": 2291.62, "text": " And then we spend millions of dollars building complex logging, auditing, and backup systems to try and recover what we just intentionally destroyed.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 550, 321, 3496, 6803, 295, 3808, 2390, 3997, 27991, 11, 2379, 1748, 11, 293, 14807, 3652, 281, 853, 293, 8114, 437, 321, 445, 22062, 8937, 13, 50718], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09674208023968865, "compression_ratio": 1.6254980079681276, "no_speech_prob": 1.6619814248788889e-12}, {"id": 554, "seek": 228456, "start": 2291.7999999999997, "end": 2292.34, "text": " Oh, absolutely.", "tokens": [50727, 876, 11, 3122, 13, 50754], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09674208023968865, "compression_ratio": 1.6254980079681276, "no_speech_prob": 1.6619814248788889e-12}, {"id": 555, "seek": 228456, "start": 2292.64, "end": 2298.56, "text": " Imagine building an operating system where the foundational kernel operates on these sphere-pop primitives natively.", "tokens": [50769, 11739, 2390, 364, 7447, 1185, 689, 264, 32195, 28256, 22577, 322, 613, 16687, 12, 13872, 2886, 38970, 8470, 356, 13, 51065], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09674208023968865, "compression_ratio": 1.6254980079681276, "no_speech_prob": 1.6619814248788889e-12}, {"id": 556, "seek": 228456, "start": 2299.04, "end": 2306.58, "text": " If we embrace the view that nothing is ever truly deleted, that consumption is just a refusal layered over an intact history,", "tokens": [51089, 759, 321, 14038, 264, 1910, 300, 1825, 307, 1562, 4908, 22981, 11, 300, 12126, 307, 445, 257, 48948, 34666, 670, 364, 23493, 2503, 11, 51466], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09674208023968865, "compression_ratio": 1.6254980079681276, "no_speech_prob": 1.6619814248788889e-12}, {"id": 557, "seek": 230658, "start": 2306.58, "end": 2315.5, "text": " imagine an OS where undo isn't a fragile, memory-heavy hack, but the fundamental, mathematically sound law of the universe.", "tokens": [50365, 3811, 364, 12731, 689, 23779, 1943, 380, 257, 23847, 11, 4675, 12, 37157, 10339, 11, 457, 264, 8088, 11, 44003, 1626, 2101, 295, 264, 6445, 13, 50811], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13189739982287088, "compression_ratio": 1.5627118644067797, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171581218235587e-12}, {"id": 558, "seek": 230658, "start": 2315.72, "end": 2317.46, "text": " A truly persistent environment.", "tokens": [50822, 316, 4908, 24315, 2823, 13, 50909], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13189739982287088, "compression_ratio": 1.5627118644067797, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171581218235587e-12}, {"id": 559, "seek": 230658, "start": 2317.7, "end": 2317.94, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50921, 1779, 13, 50933], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13189739982287088, "compression_ratio": 1.5627118644067797, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171581218235587e-12}, {"id": 560, "seek": 230658, "start": 2318.16, "end": 2322.7, "text": " What if every choice you ever made on your computer was still perfectly preserved just beneath the surface?", "tokens": [50944, 708, 498, 633, 3922, 291, 1562, 1027, 322, 428, 3820, 390, 920, 6239, 22242, 445, 17149, 264, 3753, 30, 51171], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13189739982287088, "compression_ratio": 1.5627118644067797, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171581218235587e-12}, {"id": 561, "seek": 230658, "start": 2322.7, "end": 2332.94, "text": " You could instantly roll back the entire state of your machine or branch a running application into two parallel realities with zero memory overhead just by shifting the topological pointer.", "tokens": [51171, 509, 727, 13518, 3373, 646, 264, 2302, 1785, 295, 428, 3479, 420, 9819, 257, 2614, 3861, 666, 732, 8952, 27785, 365, 4018, 4675, 19922, 445, 538, 17573, 264, 1192, 4383, 23918, 13, 51683], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13189739982287088, "compression_ratio": 1.5627118644067797, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171581218235587e-12}, {"id": 562, "seek": 233294, "start": 2332.94, "end": 2340.2400000000002, "text": " It changes computing from a destructive act of constant overriding into an exploratory act of flawlessly navigating history.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 2962, 15866, 490, 257, 26960, 605, 295, 5754, 670, 81, 2819, 666, 364, 24765, 4745, 605, 295, 13717, 12048, 32054, 2503, 13, 50730], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.163187964487884, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 2.036283251391491e-12}, {"id": 563, "seek": 233294, "start": 2340.98, "end": 2345.94, "text": " It elevates the concept of version control from a developer tool into the fundamental law of the digital universe.", "tokens": [50767, 467, 7701, 1024, 264, 3410, 295, 3037, 1969, 490, 257, 10754, 2290, 666, 264, 8088, 2101, 295, 264, 4562, 6445, 13, 51015], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.163187964487884, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 2.036283251391491e-12}, {"id": 564, "seek": 233294, "start": 2346.38, "end": 2348.82, "text": " It's an incredibly powerful paradigm to consider.", "tokens": [51037, 467, 311, 364, 6252, 4005, 24709, 281, 1949, 13, 51159], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.163187964487884, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 2.036283251391491e-12}, {"id": 565, "seek": 233294, "start": 2349.14, "end": 2351.26, "text": " It certainly makes you look at your own code differently.", "tokens": [51175, 467, 3297, 1669, 291, 574, 412, 428, 1065, 3089, 7614, 13, 51281], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.163187964487884, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 2.036283251391491e-12}, {"id": 566, "seek": 233294, "start": 2352.02, "end": 2359.12, "text": " The next time you write a line that mutates a variable or deletes a record, you have to wonder what realities you are unnecessarily destroying.", "tokens": [51319, 440, 958, 565, 291, 2464, 257, 1622, 300, 5839, 1024, 257, 7006, 420, 1103, 37996, 257, 2136, 11, 291, 362, 281, 2441, 437, 27785, 291, 366, 16799, 3289, 19926, 13, 51674], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.163187964487884, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 2.036283251391491e-12}, {"id": 567, "seek": 233294, "start": 2359.78, "end": 2361.78, "text": " Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.", "tokens": [51707, 1044, 291, 337, 5549, 505, 322, 341, 2452, 9192, 13, 51807], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.163187964487884, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 2.036283251391491e-12}, {"id": 568, "seek": 236178, "start": 2361.78, "end": 2364.6200000000003, "text": " Keep questioning the systems you interact with.", "tokens": [50365, 5527, 21257, 264, 3652, 291, 4648, 365, 13, 50507], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16780821979045868, "compression_ratio": 1.3277310924369747, "no_speech_prob": 2.973832334898785e-12}, {"id": 569, "seek": 236178, "start": 2365.2000000000003, "end": 2370.5800000000004, "text": " Look for the primitives beneath the complexity and keep exploring the invisible geometry of the digital world.", "tokens": [50536, 2053, 337, 264, 2886, 38970, 17149, 264, 14024, 293, 1066, 12736, 264, 14603, 18426, 295, 264, 4562, 1002, 13, 50805], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16780821979045868, "compression_ratio": 1.3277310924369747, "no_speech_prob": 2.973832334898785e-12}], "language": "en"}
